const TEACH={"T4":{"clue":"Late-peaking, dagger-shaped CW Doppler envelope in a tachycardic, septic patient.","mech":"An underfilled, hyperdynamic LV obliterates its cavity in late systole, so the outflow gradient builds late and the velocity peaks late.","confirm":"Walk the PW sample from the apex to the LVOT to localise the step-up; look for SAM and a small end-systolic cavity.","trap":"Do not call severe MR from peak velocity alone: MR is rounded, earlier-peaking and starts with isovolumic contraction."},"L2_8":{"clue":"Tissue Doppler targets myocardium, which moves slowly but reflects strongly.","mech":"Myocardium is dense and moves at roughly 5-20 cm/s, so its echoes are strong; blood cells are weak scatterers moving fast. TDI filters out high-velocity, low-amplitude blood signal.","confirm":"Place a small PW gate on the septal or lateral mitral annulus (or lateral tricuspid annulus), lower the scale and gain, and read e', a' and s'.","trap":"Blood flow Doppler is the high-velocity, low-amplitude signal; picking low amplitude confuses the two. Remember: tissue is slow and loud, blood is fast and quiet."},"L2_9":{"clue":"Shunt flow across a PFO is low velocity, so the colour scale must be lowered.","mech":"Colour Doppler only displays velocities within the set Nyquist range; at a valve-regurgitation scale of 50-70 cm/s, slow interatrial flow falls below display and appears absent.","confirm":"Lower the Nyquist limit to about 20 cm/s, narrow the colour box to keep frame rate, and confirm with an agitated saline bubble study.","trap":"Keeping the standard 50-70 cm/s scale is correct for regurgitant jets but hides low-velocity shunts; baseline shift alone does not reveal slow flow."},"L2_10":{"clue":"Qp is the pulmonary (RV outflow) stroke volume and Qs the systemic (LVOT) stroke volume; the ratio is pulmonary over systemic.","mech":"Stroke volume at any orifice equals cross-sectional area times VTI (continuity principle). Comparing RVOT/PA flow with LVOT flow quantifies net shunt.","confirm":"Qp:Qs = (0.785 x D_PA squared x VTI_PA) / (0.785 x D_LVOT squared x VTI_LVOT); a ratio above 1.5 suggests a significant left-to-right shunt.","trap":"Option B inverts the ratio; VTI ratios alone ignore the different diameters, and heart rate cancels so adds nothing."},"L2_11":{"clue":"The 2018 ASE statement retained only known hypersensitivity as a contraindication to ultrasound-enhancing agents.","mech":"Reactions are rare anaphylactoid (complement-mediated) events; earlier black-box cautions for ACS, pulmonary hypertension and critical illness were removed after safety data showed no excess harm.","confirm":"Screen for prior reaction to the agent or its components (e.g. PEG), use low MI imaging, and have resuscitation available.","trap":"ACS, ventilation and pulmonary hypertension were old FDA warnings now withdrawn; ICU patients are among those who benefit most from contrast."},"L3_1":{"clue":"The artifact places an echo where no structure lies on the central beam axis.","mech":"Side lobes are off-axis beams of acoustic energy; strong reflectors they hit are displayed as if on the main axis, producing spurious echoes alongside real structures.","confirm":"Image from another window or angle; a true structure persists and has anatomical continuity, whereas a side-lobe echo shifts or vanishes. Harmonics reduce side lobes.","trap":"Reverberation violates the travel-time (single reflection) assumption, not the on-axis assumption; range ambiguity violates the one-pulse-at-a-time assumption."},"L3_2":{"clue":"Equally spaced, depth-fading copies that move exactly with the structure in front.","mech":"Sound bounces repeatedly between two strong reflectors (or reflector and transducer); each round trip adds time, so copies appear at multiples of the reflector depth with decreasing intensity.","confirm":"Check that spacing equals the original reflector depth, change angle or window, and look for independent motion; real thrombus moves on its own and is seen in multiple planes.","trap":"Thrombus is the tempting dangerous diagnosis, but a real mass has independent motion and does not repeat at regular intervals."},"L3_3":{"clue":"A jet in an implausible location with identical timing and Doppler profile to a real paravalvular jet, near a strong reflector (mechanical valve).","mech":"A strong specular reflector redirects the beam; the machine assumes straight-line travel and places a duplicate image of the true jet on the far side of the reflector.","confirm":"Image in an off-axis or orthogonal plane; the mirror image disappears or moves while the true jet remains anchored to its origin.","trap":"VSD, Gerbode and ruptured sinus are real diagnoses but would have their own distinct timing, origin and velocity rather than an exact copy."},"L3_4":{"clue":"The question asks which single setting suppresses side lobes and near-field clutter.","mech":"Harmonic signals are generated within tissue and are strongest along the central high-intensity beam; weak side lobes and near-field reverberations generate little harmonic energy, so they are filtered out.","confirm":"Turn on tissue harmonic imaging, then optimise focus, TGC, depth and frequency; use contrast for persistent apical clutter.","trap":"Higher gain or MI increases artifact; widening the sector lowers frame rate and line density without removing clutter."},"L3_5":{"clue":"Brighter tissue lies behind a fluid-filled structure such as the descending aorta.","mech":"Fluid attenuates sound less than surrounding tissue, so more energy reaches deeper structures, but TGC still compensates as if tissue were present, making distal echoes bright.","confirm":"Recognise the bright band directly behind the fluid; reduce TGC in that zone to normalise brightness.","trap":"Shadowing is the opposite: dark dropout behind strongly attenuating or reflecting structures like calcium or prosthetic material."},"L3_6":{"clue":"Hazy apical echo in the near field that does not move with the wall.","mech":"Near-field clutter arises from reverberation and side lobes close to the transducer; a real thrombus is anchored to akinetic myocardium and moves with it.","confirm":"Move focus to the apex, use harmonics, adjust TGC and depth, then give an ultrasound-enhancing agent: a true thrombus appears as a filling defect.","trap":"Anticoagulating or ordering TEE first skips cheap optimisation; TEE images the apex poorly, and starting anticoagulation on artifact exposes the patient to bleeding."},"L3_7":{"clue":"Structures posterior and near the oesophagus: LAA, mitral valve, interatrial septum, thoracic aorta.","mech":"TEE sits directly behind the left atrium, giving short distance, high frequency and no lung or rib interference for posterior structures.","confirm":"For LAA thrombus, endocarditis, PFO or aortic dissection, choose TEE; for apex, RV and anterior structures, TTE is often better.","trap":"Apex, RV free wall, anterior pericardium and right-sided valves are anterior structures in the TEE far field and are usually better seen on TTE."},"L3_8":{"clue":"Unexpected high-velocity diastolic signal on a CW tracing aimed at TR, in a patient with mitral stenosis.","mech":"CW Doppler has no range resolution; it records every velocity along the whole beam, so an adjacent MS jet can be superimposed on the TR recording.","confirm":"Check timing against the ECG (MS is diastolic, TR systolic) and interrogate from another window; use PW or colour to localise the source.","trap":"Aliasing does not occur with CW, and TR or pulmonary hypertension produce systolic, not diastolic, signals."},"L3_9":{"clue":"Need to boost deep signals selectively without altering the near field.","mech":"Sound attenuates with depth, so late-returning echoes are weaker; TGC applies depth-specific amplification to equalise brightness.","confirm":"Adjust TGC sliders at the relevant depth until the myocardium appears uniformly bright from near to far field.","trap":"Overall gain amplifies all depths equally; dynamic range changes contrast, and sector width changes field of view and frame rate."},"L3_10":{"clue":"Sudden bright irregular streaks during surgery or with an LVAD.","mech":"Electrocautery and electrically active devices emit radiofrequency noise picked up by the transducer and system, displayed as streaks or bands unrelated to anatomy.","confirm":"Note the timing with cautery use or the device; the pattern disappears when cautery stops and does not respect anatomical boundaries.","trap":"Side lobes and mirror images are acoustic artifacts tied to real reflectors; they do not appear suddenly with cautery."},"L4_1":{"clue":"Angling within the same imaging plane toward or away from the indicator, without moving the probe.","mech":"Rocking keeps the plane fixed and shifts the beam sideways within it, useful for centring a structure; tilting sweeps the plane across the body.","confirm":"Practise from PLAX: rocking toward the indicator moves the image to the side of the screen; tilting moves you through base-to-apex slices.","trap":"Tilting (fanning) is the commonest confusion: it changes angle out of plane. Rotation turns around the probe axis; sliding moves the footprint."},"L4_2":{"clue":"Going from PLAX to PSAX at the same window.","mech":"The short axis is orthogonal to the long axis, so a 90 degree clockwise rotation moves the indicator from right shoulder to left shoulder, cutting the LV in cross-section.","confirm":"Rotate clockwise about 90 degrees, then tilt from the aortic valve level down through mitral, papillary and apical levels; round LV confirms on-axis.","trap":"Counter-clockwise rotation produces an off-axis or inverted image; sliding or tilting alone does not change the long axis to short axis."},"L4_3":{"clue":"LVOT VTI must be measured in laminar flow just proximal to the valve.","mech":"Stroke volume equals LVOT area times VTI at the same site; sampling in the acceleration zone or at the leaflets records higher velocities and overestimates SV.","confirm":"Apical 5- or 3-chamber, 3-5 mm gate on the LV side of the valve; look for a narrow smooth envelope with a closing click, and trace the modal velocity.","trap":"Sampling at the leaflet tips or aorta captures flow acceleration; the mid-LV cavity samples low, non-representative velocities."},"L4_4":{"clue":"Imaging the aortic arch and its branches on TTE.","mech":"The suprasternal notch window looks down through the mediastinum along the arch, avoiding lung and sternum.","confirm":"Place the probe in the notch with the neck extended and indicator toward the left jaw; identify the arch and three branch take-offs, and sample descending aorta flow.","trap":"Subcostal and apical views do not reach the arch; right parasternal views show the ascending aorta but not branch origins."},"L4_5":{"clue":"Long-axis display of RVOT, pulmonic valve and main PA.","mech":"From PLAX, tilting anteriorly and superiorly toward the left shoulder sweeps the plane onto the RVOT, which crosses anterior to the aortic root.","confirm":"Obtain the RV outflow view from PLAX or use basal PSAX; confirm the pulmonic valve and PA bifurcation and place PW for RVOT acceleration time.","trap":"Apical 3- and 2-chamber views image the left heart; the subcostal IVC view shows venous inflow, not the outflow tract."},"L4_6":{"clue":"Apical 3-chamber equals an apical PLAX, including the LVOT and aortic valve.","mech":"The plane passes through the LV long axis and the aortic valve, so it shows the anteroseptal and inferolateral walls like PLAX.","confirm":"From A4C rotate counter-clockwise about 120 degrees until the LVOT and aortic valve open out; check walls match PLAX segments.","trap":"A2C shows anterior and inferior walls without the aorta; A4C shows inferoseptum and anterolateral wall plus the right heart."},"L4_7":{"clue":"Pulmonary vein Doppler in A4C requires the vein most aligned with the beam.","mech":"The right upper pulmonary vein enters the LA nearly parallel to the apical ultrasound beam, minimising angle error and allowing a clean S, D and Ar recording.","confirm":"Use colour to find the RUPV inflow near the interatrial septum, place a 2-3 mm gate 1-2 cm into the vein and record S, D and Ar.","trap":"Left-sided veins enter at a steeper angle in A4C; the ostium contains LA flow contamination; the IVC is systemic venous flow."},"L4_8":{"clue":"Reflector moving toward the transducer.","mech":"Motion toward the source compresses wavefronts, shortening wavelength and raising received frequency (positive Doppler shift), displayed above the baseline.","confirm":"Doppler shift = 2 x f0 x v x cos(theta) / c; flow toward the transducer is positive and red on standard colour maps.","trap":"Higher frequency with longer wavelength is physically impossible at constant speed of sound; lower frequency and longer wavelength describe motion away."},"L5_1":{"clue":"Mechanically ventilated ICU patients frequently have poor transthoracic windows.","mech":"Hyperinflation, positive pressure, obesity, oedema, dressings, drains and supine positioning interpose air or tissue between probe and heart, degrading TTE in roughly half.","confirm":"When TTE is non-diagnostic for the clinical question, proceed to TEE, which adds new diagnoses in a substantial proportion of ICU patients.","trap":"Assuming most ventilated patients image well (10-25%) underestimates the problem; 90% overstates it, as many studies remain diagnostic."},"L5_2":{"clue":"TR velocity and LV apex are the stated items; TR needs beam alignment and the apex is far field on TEE.","mech":"TEE views the heart from behind, placing the apex far from the probe and often foreshortened; TR jets are commonly poorly aligned with mid-oesophageal beams.","confirm":"Use TTE apical, parasternal RV inflow and subcostal views to align CW with TR and to assess the apex with contrast if needed.","trap":"TEE is superior for dissection, LAA thrombus and localised post-cardiotomy tamponade, which are the distractors."},"L5_3":{"clue":"Upper GI perforation is the rare, serious TEE complication; overall complications are more frequent.","mech":"Perforation results from mechanical trauma, mainly at the hypopharynx and upper oesophagus during insertion, and is rarer than minor injury or bleeding.","confirm":"Before TEE, screen for oesophageal pathology, varices and dysphagia; insert gently with jaw thrust and never force against resistance.","trap":"1 in 500 is the overall complication rate, not perforation; 1 in 50 overstates risk and 1 in 500,000 dismisses it."},"L5_4":{"clue":"Anteflexion and retroflexion of the probe tip.","mech":"The large wheel controls the anterior-posterior flexion cables; the small wheel controls left-right lateral flexion.","confirm":"Before insertion, check wheel function and locks on your probe; use slight anteflexion around the tongue base and keep wheels unlocked during advancement.","trap":"Rotating the probe turns the plane left or right, and advancing or withdrawing changes level; neither flexes the tip."},"L5_5":{"clue":"In mid-oesophageal views the chamber at the top of the sector is the one adjacent to the oesophagus.","mech":"The left atrium lies directly anterior to the oesophagus, so it is in the near field at the apex of the TEE sector.","confirm":"On ME 4C, confirm LA at the top, then LV, with RA and RV toward the right of the screen; this orientation anchors all ME views.","trap":"The RV and RA are anterior structures, far from the probe; transthoracic orientation habits mislead here."},"L5_6":{"clue":"ME 4-chamber is the TEE analogue of A4C.","mech":"The plane cuts the heart through both atria and ventricles in the same orientation as A4C, displaying the inferoseptum and anterolateral wall.","confirm":"At about 0-20 degrees, retroflex slightly to avoid foreshortening and identify the inferoseptum and anterolateral wall for regional analysis.","trap":"ME LAX matches PLAX/A3C (anteroseptal and inferolateral), and ME 2C matches A2C (anterior and inferior); mixing these mismatches coronary territories."},"L5_7":{"clue":"SVC, IVC, fossa ovalis, Eustachian valve and RA appendage in one image.","mech":"At about 90-110 degrees turned to the right, the plane runs along the superior-inferior axis of the right atrium, aligning both cavae with the interatrial septum.","confirm":"Turn the probe rightward from ME 2C at 90-110 degrees; use this view for bubble studies, ECMO cannula position and SVC collapsibility.","trap":"ME AV short axis shows the septum and RA but not both cavae; transgastric and UE arch views are unrelated."},"L5_8":{"clue":"Transgastric mid-papillary SAX displays all three coronary territories at once.","mech":"The cross-section through the papillary muscles includes segments supplied by LAD, RCA and circumflex, and the LV cavity size reflects filling.","confirm":"Use it to track LV end-diastolic area, global contraction and regional wall motion during haemodynamic interventions.","trap":"Aortic gradients need deep transgastric views; PFO needs bicaval; descending aorta needs probe rotation posteriorly."},"L5_9":{"clue":"TEE probe stays in place without the operator touching the chest.","mech":"Continuous imaging during compressions allows rhythm and motion assessment within brief pauses, and visualising the compressed region guides hand position.","confirm":"Pre-position at ME 4C or ME LAX before the pulse check; record during the check and review while compressions continue.","trap":"TTE requires access to the chest and often prolongs pauses; TEE does not prolong pauses or prevent compression assessment."},"L6_1":{"clue":"ASE recommends a volumetric 2D method for EF.","mech":"Biplane method of disks sums elliptical disks from two orthogonal planes, so it accounts for regional variation and requires fewer geometric assumptions than linear methods.","confirm":"Trace endocardium at end-diastole and end-systole in A4C and A2C, avoid foreshortening, and compute EF = (EDV - ESV)/EDV.","trap":"Teichholz and fractional shortening assume symmetric geometry and fail with regional wall motion abnormalities; EPSS is a surrogate, not a measurement."},"L6_2":{"clue":"LVEDD 55 mm and LVESD 41 mm in PLAX.","mech":"Fractional shortening expresses the percentage reduction of the LV minor-axis diameter during systole, a linear index of radial function.","confirm":"FS = (55 - 41)/55 = 14/55 = 25%; measure at the mitral leaflet tips perpendicular to the long axis.","trap":"14% is the absolute difference in mm mistaken for a percentage; 34% and 41% come from dividing by the wrong diameter."},"L6_3":{"clue":"EF 38% lies in the 30-40% band.","mech":"ASE 2015 categories: normal 52-72% men and 54-74% women, mildly abnormal 41-51/53%, moderately abnormal 30-40%, severely abnormal below 30%.","confirm":"Confirm by biplane disks; if image quality is poor, use contrast to improve endocardial definition before classifying.","trap":"Heart-failure EF categories (HFmrEF 41-49%) differ from echo severity grading; do not label 38% as only mildly reduced."},"L6_4":{"clue":"Increased distance between the anterior mitral leaflet E-point and the septum.","mech":"A dilated, poorly contracting LV with low transmitral flow opens the mitral leaflet less and moves the septum away, widening the gap.","confirm":"M-mode through anterior leaflet tip in PLAX; EPSS above 7 mm suggests EF below 30%. Exclude MS, AR jets and septal wall motion abnormalities.","trap":"Normal EPSS is under about 6 mm; thresholds of 2-4 mm would label normal hearts abnormal, and 15 mm is far too insensitive."},"L6_5":{"clue":"MAPSE threshold in a woman for normal EF.","mech":"Longitudinal fibres shorten the LV toward the apex; mitral annular excursion reflects longitudinal systolic function and correlates with global EF.","confirm":"M-mode through the lateral mitral annulus in A4C: at least 11 mm (women) or 13 mm (men) suggests EF above 55%; below 6 mm suggests severe dysfunction.","trap":"Using the male threshold for a woman, or a TAPSE-like value, misclassifies; MAPSE is angle-dependent and foreshortening underestimates it."},"L6_6":{"clue":"Fractional area change of the LV in PSAX.","mech":"FAC compares end-diastolic and end-systolic cavity areas, approximating radial contraction in one plane.","confirm":"FAC = (EDA - ESA)/EDA x 100 at mid-papillary level; normal about 35-65%.","trap":"Values of 20-30% indicate dysfunction, and 70-90% suggests hyperdynamic or underfilled ventricles rather than normal."},"L6_7":{"clue":"Apex not fully included in apical views.","mech":"A foreshortened plane cuts the LV obliquely short of the true apex, shortening the long axis and reducing traced volumes.","confirm":"Look for an elongated, tapered apex; move the probe laterally and inferiorly to find the true apex, and compare long-axis length between A4C and A2C.","trap":"Endocardial dropout causes overestimation, the opposite; foreshortening also often makes the apex look hypercontractile and overestimates EF."},"L6_8":{"clue":"Wall thickness measurement location and timing.","mech":"At end-diastole the LV is at maximal volume and walls are thinnest; standardising timing and level makes thickness reproducible and comparable with normal ranges.","confirm":"Measure in PLAX perpendicular to the LV long axis at the mitral leaflet tips, at the onset of QRS or largest LV dimension, excluding RV trabeculae.","trap":"End-systolic measurements are thicker and not comparable with norms; A4C does not show the inferolateral wall."},"L6_9":{"clue":"LVOT diameter is squared in the CSA formula.","mech":"CSA = pi x (D/2) squared; a 10% error in diameter produces about a 21% error in area and therefore SV.","confirm":"Zoom on the LVOT in mid-systole, measure inner edge to inner edge at the annulus, and keep the same diameter for serial SV tracking.","trap":"VTI tracing and a 10 degree angle (cosine error about 1.5%) matter less; heart rate does not affect SV calculation."},"L6_10":{"clue":"An ICU patient whose EF improved while on norepinephrine and hypovolaemic.","mech":"EF depends on preload, afterload and contractility; catecholamines and low preload change EF without reflecting intrinsic myocardial recovery.","confirm":"Integrate EF with SV/LVOT VTI, filling pressures, LV size and the response to fluid or vasoactive changes to decide whether the heart limits perfusion.","trap":"Chasing a more precise EF or calling it normal misses the critical-care question: is the heart contributing to the current haemodynamic problem?"},"L7_1":{"clue":"Diastole defined physiologically, not electrically.","mech":"Diastole begins when the LV stops ejecting (aortic valve closure) and ends when ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure (mitral valve closure), including IVRT, rapid filling, diastasis and atrial contraction.","confirm":"Mark aortic closure and mitral closure on the Doppler or M-mode traces to identify IVRT and filling periods.","trap":"ECG intervals approximate timing but are not mechanical; MV closure to AV opening is isovolumic contraction, which is systole."},"L7_2":{"clue":"Reduced EF, regional wall motion abnormality or pathological LVH.","mech":"Each of these implies myocardial disease, which virtually always impairs relaxation, so the question becomes filling pressure rather than whether dysfunction exists.","confirm":"Move directly to the ASE 2016 filling-pressure algorithm: E/A and E, average E/e', TR velocity and LAVI.","trap":"A small LA and normal EF without LVH point toward the normal-EF algorithm; IVC size estimates RAP, not LV relaxation."},"L7_3":{"clue":"LA enlargement is sensitive but has non-diastolic causes.","mech":"LA volume reflects chronic exposure to raised pressure or volume, which also occurs in AF, high-output states, athletes and mitral valve disease.","confirm":"Interpret LAVI alongside E/e', TR velocity and mitral inflow; check rhythm, valve disease and output status before attributing it to diastolic dysfunction.","trap":"Hypovolaemia shrinks the LA; pericardial effusion and acute MI are not chronic LA dilators."},"L7_4":{"clue":"E/A 1.2 with E 85 cm/s in an abnormal LV; E/e' 16, TR 3.0 m/s, LAVI 40 mL/m2.","mech":"With intermediate E/A, three criteria arbitrate: E/e' above 14, TR above 2.8 m/s and LAVI above 34. Three of three positive indicates elevated LAP.","confirm":"Count criteria: 16 above 14 (positive), 3.0 above 2.8 (positive), 40 above 34 (positive): 3/3 elevated LAP, grade II.","trap":"Grade III needs E/A at least 2; indeterminate applies only when the criteria split 1:1 with one unavailable."},"L7_5":{"clue":"E/A 0.7 with E 45 cm/s.","mech":"Impaired relaxation shifts filling to late diastole (low E, dominant A); a low E with E/A at most 0.8 indicates LAP is not elevated.","confirm":"Apply the rule: E/A at most 0.8 and E at most 50 cm/s means grade I with normal LAP; no further criteria needed.","trap":"If E/A at most 0.8 but E is above 50 cm/s, criteria must be applied; reading E/A alone without E leads to errors."},"L7_6":{"clue":"E/A 2.3 with EF 25%.","mech":"High LAP drives a vigorous early E, while a stiff LV causes rapid equalisation (short DT) and a small A; this restrictive pattern indicates elevated filling pressures.","confirm":"Confirm with short DT (below about 160 ms), high E/e', TR velocity and enlarged LA; check response to preload reduction.","trap":"In young healthy people E/A above 2 can be normal, but with reduced EF it is restrictive; grade II requires E/A below 2."},"L7_7":{"clue":"E/e' below 8 versus above 15.","mech":"E rises with LAP, whereas e' reflects relaxation and is relatively load-independent; dividing E by e' corrects for relaxation and estimates filling pressure.","confirm":"Measure E and e' (average septal and lateral); below 8 suggests normal PCWP, above 14-15 suggests elevated.","trap":"Reversing the cut-offs is a common slip; values between 8 and 14 are indeterminate and need other criteria."},"L7_8":{"clue":"E 55 cm/s, e' 8 cm/s, TR 2.6 m/s in a dyspnoeic BiPAP patient.","mech":"E/e' 55/8 = 6.9 suggests low LAP; TR velocity below 2.8 m/s shows no pulmonary pressure elevation. Infiltrates are therefore unlikely cardiogenic.","confirm":"Calculate E/e' = 6.9 (normal), check TR below 2.8 m/s, and look for lung ultrasound signs of pneumonia; avoid diuresis on these numbers.","trap":"EF 47% does not mean high filling pressures; BiPAP does not prevent Doppler assessment."},"L7_9":{"clue":"Very low CO and SV with E/e' 5 and low E (grade I pattern).","mech":"Low E/e' and normal LAVI indicate low filling pressure; output is limited by contractility and preload, so removing volume would further reduce SV.","confirm":"Calculate CO (SV x HR) and confirm low LAP; consider careful small fluid challenges with SV monitoring plus inotropic or mechanical support.","trap":"Reflex diuresis in severe HFrEF is the trap; low output with low filling pressure is not congestion."},"L7_10":{"clue":"Conditions that confound mitral inflow or annular velocities.","mech":"AF removes the A wave and varies E; MAC and MV disease alter e' and inflow; regional dysfunction makes septal and lateral e' unrepresentative; constriction raises medial e'.","confirm":"Use alternative indices (TR velocity, LAVI, E/e' averaged over several beats, pulmonary vein flow) when these conditions are present.","trap":"Normal EF and young age are not limitations; the algorithm specifically includes normal-EF patients."},"L8_1":{"clue":"RV larger than two-thirds of the LV but still smaller than the LV in A4C.","mech":"The normal RV is crescentic and smaller than the LV; progressive enlargement is graded by comparing RV and LV size in the same image.","confirm":"Use an RV-focused A4C and measure basal diameter (above 41 mm abnormal) and RV/LV ratio; avoid foreshortened views.","trap":"Moderate enlargement is RV equal to LV and severe is RV larger than LV; overcalling happens when the A4C is rotated to exaggerate RV size."},"L8_2":{"clue":"RV basal diameter cut-off in an RV-focused A4C.","mech":"ASE/EACVI 2015 upper limits: basal 41 mm, mid 35 mm, longitudinal 83 mm; values above these indicate dilation.","confirm":"Use an RV-focused A4C with the apex centred; measure basal diameter in the basal third at end-diastole.","trap":"28 mm and 20 mm are below normal ranges; 60 mm longitudinal is within normal limits."},"L8_3":{"clue":"RV free-wall thickness 7 mm.","mech":"Free-wall thickness above 5 mm reflects hypertrophy from chronic pressure overload or infiltration; the RV cannot thicken within days.","confirm":"Measure at end-diastole in the subcostal view below the tricuspid annulus, excluding epicardial fat and trabeculae; above 10 mm is severe.","trap":"Acute cor pulmonale produces a dilated thin-walled RV; epicardial fat or effusion can be mistaken for wall thickness."},"L8_4":{"clue":"Dilated thin-walled RV with RVSP below 60 mm Hg.","mech":"A normal RV cannot acutely generate systolic pressures above about 60 mm Hg; chronic overload produces hypertrophy that allows higher pressures.","confirm":"Measure RV wall thickness (5 mm or less), RVSP by TR jet, and assess RA size; high pressure plus RVH points to a chronic process.","trap":"RVSP 85 mm Hg indicates chronicity, not acute failure; RA dilation alone is nonspecific."},"L8_5":{"clue":"TAPSE threshold for RV dysfunction.","mech":"TAPSE measures longitudinal excursion of the lateral tricuspid annulus, the dominant contraction direction of the RV.","confirm":"M-mode through the lateral tricuspid annulus in A4C with the cursor aligned to motion; below 17 mm indicates dysfunction. Cross-check with S' below 9.5 cm/s and FAC below 35%.","trap":"TAPSE is angle- and load-dependent and is reduced after cardiac surgery despite normal RV EF; 20-25 mm are normal values."},"L8_6":{"clue":"IVC 1.8 cm with less than 50% collapse.","mech":"IVC size and collapse reflect RA pressure; a small IVC with poor collapse is a mixed picture, so RAP is intermediate.","confirm":"Apply the ASE scheme: at most 2.1 cm and above 50% collapse = 3 mm Hg; above 2.1 cm and below 50% collapse = 15; otherwise 8 mm Hg.","trap":"Using diameter alone (small = 3) ignores the poor collapse; the ventilated-patient caveats do not apply here because breathing is spontaneous."},"L8_7":{"clue":"Small IVC below 12 mm in a ventilated patient.","mech":"Positive pressure raises intrathoracic pressure and distends the IVC; even so, a small IVC indicates low RA pressure, making it highly specific.","confirm":"Measure IVC 1-2 cm from the RA junction at end-expiration; below 12 mm strongly supports RAP below 10 mm Hg.","trap":"A large IVC under ventilation does not indicate high RAP; collapse above 50% applies to spontaneous breathing, and distensibility predicts fluid response, not RAP."},"L8_8":{"clue":"Tricuspid E/e' at least 4.7 in sepsis.","mech":"Analogous to mitral E/e', the ratio corrects tricuspid inflow for RV relaxation, estimating right atrial pressure.","confirm":"Measure tricuspid E with PW at leaflet tips and lateral tricuspid annulus e'; a ratio at least 4.7 suggests RAP at least 10 mm Hg.","trap":"It estimates pressure, not fluid responsiveness; it does not reflect left-sided PCWP."},"L8_9":{"clue":"TR Vmax 4.04 m/s and RAP 15 mm Hg.","mech":"Simplified Bernoulli converts peak TR velocity to the systolic RV-RA gradient; adding RAP gives RVSP.","confirm":"RVSP = 4 x 4.04 squared + 15 = 4 x 16.3 + 15 = 65 + 15 = 80 mm Hg.","trap":"Forgetting to add RAP gives 65; failing to square velocity gives about 31; doubling instead of squaring leads astray."},"L8_10":{"clue":"Prolonged bypass, poor myocardial protection and air to the RCA.","mech":"The RCA arises anteriorly so air bubbles enter it in the supine patient; long ischaemic times and inadequate cardioplegia stun the RV.","confirm":"Assess RV size, TAPSE/S', septal motion and TR on post-bypass TEE; correlate with CVP and haemodynamics.","trap":"Off-pump surgery and short-bypass procedures reduce, not increase, RV injury risk; sternal infection is a late, unrelated complication."},"L8_11":{"clue":"RV dysfunction after ROSC.","mech":"RV dysfunction after arrest reflects pulmonary vascular injury, ischaemia or underlying PE and limits LV filling, predicting poor outcome independently of LV function.","confirm":"Assess RV size, TAPSE, septal motion and TR velocity after ROSC; look for cause (PE, infarct, ventilator afterload).","trap":"A hyperdynamic LV and small IVC suggest hypovolaemia, not prognosis; normal RVSP is reassuring."},"L9_1":{"clue":"Distinguish preload, preload responsiveness, need for fluid and fluid tolerance.","mech":"Static preload (LVEDA, CVP) does not predict where the patient sits on the Frank-Starling curve; responsiveness does not mean fluid is needed or safe.","confirm":"Before giving fluid, confirm hypoperfusion, test responsiveness (PLR with VTI) and assess tolerance (venous congestion, B-lines, RV function).","trap":"Fluid responsiveness is a normal physiological state; treating it as an indication for fluid causes overload."},"L9_2":{"clue":"Passive ventilation, Vt 10 mL/kg, regular rhythm, closed chest.","mech":"SVV relies on cyclic changes in intrathoracic pressure from controlled tidal breaths altering preload; any extra variability or low Vt invalidates it.","confirm":"Check prerequisites: passive patient, Vt 8 mL/kg or more, sinus rhythm, normal compliance, HR/RR ratio above 3.6, closed chest; then measure LVOT Vmax variation.","trap":"Low-Vt ARDS, spontaneous breathing, BiPAP and arrhythmia patients produce false negatives or false positives."},"L9_3":{"clue":"Low tidal volume or low compliance.","mech":"Small Vt or stiff lungs transmit little pressure to the pleura, so preload barely changes and SVV stays low even when the patient is fluid responsive.","confirm":"Use a tidal volume challenge (6 to 8 mL/kg) or PLR instead when Vt is low or compliance reduced.","trap":"Spontaneous efforts, arrhythmia and raised abdominal pressure create false positives, not false negatives."},"L9_4":{"clue":"IVC distensibility in passive ventilation using Barbier's index.","mech":"In passive positive-pressure ventilation the IVC distends with inspiration; a fluid-responsive patient on the steep Starling curve shows greater distension.","confirm":"Measure IVCmax and IVCmin with M-mode; distensibility = (IVCmax - IVCmin)/IVCmin x 100, above 18% predicts response.","trap":"Feissel's index uses the mean (above 12%); 50% applies to collapsibility in spontaneous breathing."},"L9_5":{"clue":"Positive-pressure inspiration effect on extrathoracic and intrathoracic veins.","mech":"Raised intrathoracic pressure increases RA pressure, impeding venous return: the extrathoracic IVC distends, while the intrathoracic SVC is compressed.","confirm":"Measure IVC distensibility (TTE) or SVC collapsibility (TEE) to assess fluid responsiveness in ventilated patients.","trap":"Spontaneous inspiration produces the opposite pattern; confusing the breathing mode reverses interpretation."},"L9_6":{"clue":"Plethoric IVC despite fluid responsiveness.","mech":"Conditions raising RA pressure or RV afterload (tamponade, RV infarction, severe TR) distend the IVC and abolish collapse regardless of volume status.","confirm":"Assess RV function and pericardium before interpreting the IVC; use dynamic SV tests such as PLR with VTI.","trap":"Extreme inspiratory effort and lateral displacement cause false positives (collapse without responsiveness), not false negatives."},"L9_7":{"clue":"PLR autotransfusion volume.","mech":"Raising the legs and lowering the trunk shifts venous blood from the lower limbs and splanchnic bed toward the heart, about 300 mL, reversibly.","confirm":"Start semi-recumbent, lower trunk and raise legs 45 degrees; measure LVOT VTI or CO within 1 minute; at least 10-15% rise = responsive.","trap":"1-2 L overestimates; PLR is a reversible challenge equivalent to a modest bolus, not a large fluid load."},"L9_8":{"clue":"Bilateral below-knee amputation, spontaneously breathing, on nasal cannula.","mech":"PLR depends on lower-limb venous volume, which is reduced after amputation; SVV and SVC tests need passive ventilation, leaving IVC collapsibility.","confirm":"Measure IVC collapsibility with M-mode during quiet breathing; thresholds of about 40-48% suggest responsiveness, with limited accuracy.","trap":"PLR is attractive but unreliable here; LVOT variation and SVC distension require mechanical ventilation."},"L9_9":{"clue":"Rapid 50 mL bolus over 10 s with VTI change.","mech":"A tiny rapid bolus raises preload slightly; on the steep Starling curve SV changes measurably, predicting response to a larger bolus.","confirm":"Measure LVOT VTI before and immediately after 50 mL crystalloid over 10 s; a rise of at least about 10% predicts responsiveness.","trap":"Larger boluses are standard challenges, not mini-challenges; PPV and IVC thresholds are different methods."},"L9_10":{"clue":"PLR with TTE requires consistent probe position and baseline return.","mech":"Small percentage changes in VTI are easily swamped by changes in probe angle or window; returning to baseline confirms the effect.","confirm":"Keep the same apical window and angle, measure VTI baseline, during PLR and after return; a reversible rise of at least 10% confirms response.","trap":"Re-measuring LVOT diameter adds error; IVC size is a static measure; starting supine flat removes the splanchnic contribution."},"L10_1":{"clue":"Four ACP risk factors in ARDS from Mekontso Dessap.","mech":"Pneumonia, high driving pressure, severe hypoxaemia and hypercapnia increase pulmonary vascular resistance and RV afterload.","confirm":"Score one point each: pneumonia, driving pressure at least 18 cm H2O, P/F below 150, PaCO2 at least 48 mm Hg; screen with echo in high scorers.","trap":"Age, lactate, prone and ECMO are tempting severity markers but are not part of the score."},"L10_2":{"clue":"ACP definition: RV dilation plus septal dyskinesia.","mech":"Acute pressure overload dilates the RV and pushes the septum toward the LV at end-systole, producing paradoxical septal motion.","confirm":"Measure RVEDA/LVEDA ratio in A4C or TEE (0.6-1.0 moderate, above 1.0 severe) and assess septal motion in PSAX.","trap":"TAPSE, RVSP or wall thickness alone are not the definition; high RVSP or thick wall suggest chronicity."},"L10_3":{"clue":"Patchy B-lines with spared areas, irregular pleura and subpleural consolidations.","mech":"ARDS is inflammatory and heterogeneous, damaging pleura and causing patchy consolidation; cardiogenic oedema is hydrostatic and homogeneous.","confirm":"Scan multiple zones bilaterally: look for spared areas, pleural irregularity, subpleural consolidations, and reduced sliding; integrate with echo filling pressures.","trap":"Diffuse homogeneous B-lines with smooth pleura favour cardiogenic oedema; A-lines and lung point indicate other diagnoses."},"L10_4":{"clue":"Vasopressor property useful when RV afterload is high.","mech":"Low-dose vasopressin acts on V1 receptors to raise SVR and may cause pulmonary vasodilation via nitric oxide, maintaining coronary perfusion without increasing PVR.","confirm":"Add vasopressin 0.03 U/min to norepinephrine, monitor RV size, septal motion and SV.","trap":"Phenylephrine increases PVR; epinephrine and dopamine cause tachyarrhythmias; they do not reduce RV afterload."},"L10_5":{"clue":"RV-protective ventilation targets.","mech":"High plateau and driving pressures compress pulmonary capillaries and raise PVR; hypercapnia and hypoxaemia cause pulmonary vasoconstriction.","confirm":"Target plateau below 30 and driving pressure below 18 cm H2O, PaCO2 below about 48 mm Hg, adequate oxygenation, and prone positioning; reassess RV on echo.","trap":"High PEEP regardless of overdistension worsens RV afterload; higher Vt and permissive hypercapnia above 60 harm the RV."},"L10_6":{"clue":"Low PCWP, modest CVP, low CO with ACP on high PEEP.","mech":"The RV needs adequate but not excessive preload; a small bolus tests whether SV rises without worsening RV dilation and septal shift.","confirm":"Give 100 mL and measure LVOT VTI and RV size; continue only if SV rises and RV dilation does not worsen. Then reduce driving pressure and consider prone.","trap":"Raising PEEP worsens RV afterload; milrinone and PDE5 inhibitors can worsen systemic hypotension."},"L10_7":{"clue":"Maintain sinus rhythm in ARDS with ACP.","mech":"The failing RV relies on atrial contraction to fill; AF reduces filling and output.","confirm":"Treat AF promptly with cardioversion or rhythm control; monitor RV function and SV after conversion.","trap":"Rate control alone or pacing at 50 bpm does not restore atrial kick; slow rates reduce output in a fixed-SV RV."},"L10_8":{"clue":"Tissue-like lung with air bronchograms.","mech":"Consolidated alveoli filled with fluid or cells transmit sound like solid tissue; air in patent bronchi creates bright branching lines.","confirm":"Observe bronchograms: dynamic movement with breathing suggests pneumonia, static suggests atelectasis; evaluate extent with colour Doppler.","trap":"Effusion is anechoic; pneumothorax shows absent sliding and A-lines; normal lung shows A-lines."},"L10_9":{"clue":"Hypoxaemia worsening with higher PEEP in ARDS with ACP.","mech":"Increased RA pressure from high PEEP and ACP opens a PFO, creating right-to-left shunt and worsening hypoxaemia.","confirm":"Perform an agitated saline bubble study, preferably TEE bicaval view, with release of a Valsalva or PEEP manoeuvre; bubbles in the LA within 3 beats confirm PFO.","trap":"Valve disease and LV thrombus do not explain PEEP-sensitive hypoxaemia; pursuing them delays recognition of the shunt."},"L10_10":{"clue":"Mortality by Berlin ARDS severity.","mech":"Severity strata reflect worsening hypoxaemia and lung injury; mortality rises across mild, moderate and severe groups.","confirm":"Classify by P/F ratio with PEEP at least 5: mild 200-300, moderate 100-200, severe below 100; mortality about 27%, 32%, 45%.","trap":"Very high mortality (40-70%) overstates mild disease; low values (5-25%) understate."},"L11_1":{"clue":"Absence of lung sliding, lung pulse and B-lines at a point.","mech":"Each of these signs requires visceral pleura contact with parietal pleura; air separating them abolishes all three while A-lines persist.","confirm":"Scan anterior chest; if all three are absent, look for the lung point to confirm pneumothorax. Use M-mode for the barcode sign.","trap":"A-lines persist in pneumothorax; ribs and pleural line remain visible; seashore sign indicates normal sliding."},"L11_2":{"clue":"Supine trauma patient: pneumothorax air collects anteriorly where a supine film sees it poorly.","mech":"In the supine chest, free air rises to the anterior, medial and basal pleural space, so no apical edge appears on an AP film. The probe on the anterior chest finds that air directly.","confirm":"Scan the least-dependent anterior zones for absent sliding, absent B-lines and lung pulse, then find a lung point. Pooled LUS sensitivity is about 91% versus about 47% for CXR.","trap":"Option D swaps the numbers. CXR is very specific but misses roughly half of supine pneumothoraces, so a normal film does not exclude one."},"L11_3":{"clue":"Absent sliding can come from any process that stops pleural movement, not only from air between the pleural layers.","mech":"Sliding needs both ventilation and two freely moving apposed pleural layers. Adhesions, bullae, mainstem intubation or obstruction, apnoea, ARDS and hyperinflation all reduce or abolish it.","confirm":"Look for lung pulse or B-lines, which prove apposition and exclude pneumothorax at that spot; only a lung point confirms pneumothorax.","trap":"Effusion and pulmonary oedema usually preserve sliding. Calling pneumothorax from absent sliding alone leads to unnecessary drains, especially with bullae or a right mainstem tube."},"L11_4":{"clue":"A hyperechoic vertical line from the pleura that moves with sliding and reaches the bottom of the screen without fading.","mech":"When the lung periphery has lost some air (fluid, cells, fibrosis), air-fluid interfaces trap sound and reverberate, producing a long vertical artifact that erases A-lines.","confirm":"Count B-lines per intercostal space: three or more defines a positive interstitial region. Note distribution: homogeneous bilateral versus patchy or focal.","trap":"Short vertical artifacts that fade within 1-2 cm are comet tails (Z-lines or I-lines) with no pathological meaning; horizontal lines at multiples of pleural depth are A-lines."},"L11_5":{"clue":"B-lines track extravascular lung water and appear early, but many diseases produce them.","mech":"Any process thickening the interstitium or partially filling alveoli (hydrostatic oedema, ARDS, pneumonitis, ILD, haemorrhage) creates B-lines before oxygenation falls.","confirm":"Count B-lines serially to trend lung water; decide cause using echo filling pressures (E/e', LA, IVC), pleural line morphology and distribution.","trap":"B-lines do not estimate PAOP, and they are not specific for cardiogenic oedema; ARDS and fibrosis also give B-lines."},"L11_6":{"clue":"Air bronchograms that move with respiration inside a consolidation.","mech":"Movement of air within the bronchi means the airway is patent and connected to ventilated lung, which is typical of infective consolidation rather than resorptive collapse.","confirm":"Watch bronchograms through several breaths; dynamic movement favours pneumonia. Static bronchograms with volume loss suggest obstructive atelectasis and may prompt bronchoscopy.","trap":"Obstructive atelectasis shows static or absent bronchograms. Infarcts are typically wedge-shaped and air-free, and effusion is fluid with no air."},"L11_7":{"clue":"Small, homogeneous, hypoechoic, wedge-shaped subpleural lesion with sharp margins and no air.","mech":"Distal to an embolus, haemorrhagic infarction fills alveoli with blood, so the lesion is airless and pleural-based in the shape of the occluded segment.","confirm":"Look for multiple peripheral lesions, often in the dorsal lower lobes with small adjacent effusion, then scan the heart and leg veins. Confirm PE with CTPA when feasible.","trap":"Pneumonia lesions are polygonal with blurred margins and air inclusions; metastases are round with sharp margins. Calling every subpleural lesion pneumonia misses PE."},"L11_8":{"clue":"Septations within an effusion.","mech":"Fibrin strands form in inflammatory exudates and organising blood; transudates have no protein or fibrin to create septations.","confirm":"Treat septations as exudate or haemothorax: sample the fluid (pH, LDH, protein, cell count), and plan a drain if infection is suspected.","trap":"Anechoic does not mean transudate: exudates and acute haemothorax can be anechoic. Size and laterality do not discriminate."},"L11_9":{"clue":"Interpleural distance 30 mm at end-expiration in a supine ventilated patient.","mech":"In the supine patient fluid layers posteriorly; the parietal-visceral separation at the base scales roughly linearly with volume.","confirm":"Balik formula: V (mL) = 20 x separation (mm) = 20 x 30 = about 600 mL. Measure at end-expiration, posterior axillary line, maximal separation.","trap":"Using a different formula or measuring at end-inspiration changes the result; 200 mL undercounts and 1,500-3,000 mL misapply the multiplier."},"L11_10":{"clue":"On M-mode, the visceral pleura moves toward the parietal line with each inspiration, producing a sine wave.","mech":"With free fluid the aerated lung expands into the effusion on inspiration, so the interpleural distance falls cyclically.","confirm":"Place the M-mode line across the effusion; a sinusoid confirms free-flowing fluid. Its absence suggests trapped lung or viscous fluid such as empyema.","trap":"The sinusoid sign is a fluid sign, not a consolidation sign; pneumothorax shows the barcode sign."},"L11_11":{"clue":"Transverse and longitudinal neck scan showing thyroid cartilage, cricoid ring and tracheal rings.","mech":"The cricothyroid membrane spans between the thyroid and cricoid cartilages; it is superficial, relatively avascular and lies below the vocal cords.","confirm":"Use the longitudinal string-of-pearls view (tracheal rings), slide cranially to the cricoid, and mark the membrane just above it before any crisis.","trap":"The thyrohyoid membrane is above the larynx and would enter at the wrong level; tracheal rings are deeper and close to the thyroid isthmus."},"L12_1":{"clue":"BLUE combines lung profiles with leg venous compression.","mech":"Lung ultrasound classifies the pattern (A-lines, B-lines, sliding, consolidation, PLAPS); venous ultrasound adds the DVT probability that identifies PE in patients with a dry A-profile.","confirm":"Scan standard BLUE points then compress femoral and popliteal veins; follow the decision tree (A-profile plus DVT = PE).","trap":"Echocardiography, IVC and diaphragm assessments are useful additions in later protocols but not the original BLUE second component."},"L12_2":{"clue":"Bilateral anterior A-lines with sliding, plus a lower-limb DVT.","mech":"An A-profile means no interstitial syndrome, so cardiogenic oedema is unlikely; dyspnoea with a dry lung and a DVT points to embolic obstruction.","confirm":"Confirm DVT by full venous compression; assess RV size and function; treat as PE or confirm with CTPA depending on stability.","trap":"A-profile plus PLAPS without DVT suggests pneumonia; A-profile without DVT or PLAPS suggests COPD or asthma. B-profile suggests cardiogenic oedema."},"L12_3":{"clue":"Pooled accuracy of lung ultrasound versus CXR for acute heart failure.","mech":"Interstitial oedema produces diffuse bilateral B-lines before radiographic changes, so LUS detects congestion earlier and more reliably.","confirm":"Look for bilateral B-line positive zones (at least two per side); pooled LUS sensitivity and specificity are both about 92%, versus CXR about 77% and 87%.","trap":"Option A gives the CXR figures; choosing them assumes the radiograph is the better test."},"L12_4":{"clue":"LUS-ARDS score cut-off above 8.","mech":"A low cut-off detects almost all ARDS patients (high sensitivity), so a score at or below 8 makes ARDS unlikely; a higher cut-off (above 27) gives specificity.","confirm":"Calculate the score from standardised regions; use above 8 to rule out and above 27 to rule in bilateral opacities under the new global definition.","trap":"High specificity belongs to the higher cut-off. The score detects bilateral aeration loss; it does not predict PEEP recruitability or diagnose pneumonia."},"L12_5":{"clue":"Change in LUS score with PEEP compared with CT-measured recruitment.","mech":"LUS sees only the subpleural lung surface; aeration changes deeper in the lung and overdistension (already fully aerated, A-lines) cannot be graded.","confirm":"Use LUS to track gross aeration; quantify recruitment with pressure-volume curves, recruitment-to-inflation ratio, EIT or CT.","trap":"Assuming fewer B-lines with PEEP proves safe recruitment ignores overdistension, which looks identical to normal aeration on ultrasound."},"L12_6":{"clue":"Rise of at least 6 B-lines across four anterior points during the SBT.","mech":"Removing positive pressure increases venous return and LV afterload; a susceptible LV raises LAP, causing rapid interstitial oedema seen as new B-lines.","confirm":"Count B-lines at four anterior points before and at the end of the SBT; an increase of 6 or more supports weaning-induced oedema. Correlate with E/A and E/e' rise.","trap":"New effusion, lung point or loss of sliding reflect other processes; a single high B-line count without comparison does not show the SBT effect."},"L12_7":{"clue":"Lateral E/e' at most 8 on TEE in ventilated patients.","mech":"Low E relative to e' indicates that the LV fills without high driving pressure, so LA pressure, and therefore PAOP, is not elevated.","confirm":"Measure lateral mitral E and e' on TEE; at most 8 predicts PAOP at most 18 mm Hg. Repeat during SBT to detect rising filling pressures.","trap":"E/e' estimates a range, not an exact pressure; reading low E/e' as PAOP at least 25 reverses the relationship."},"L12_8":{"clue":"Reduced EF with restrictive inflow, or high E/e' with normal EF, and no CO rise with PLR.","mech":"Weaning increases preload and LV afterload; a ventricle already at high filling pressure or unable to increase output decompensates into pulmonary oedema.","confirm":"Before SBT measure EF, E/A, E/e' and PLR response; after SBT look for rising E/A and E/e' and new B-lines.","trap":"Hyperdynamic EF, normal TAPSE or small IVC do not predict cardiac weaning failure; hypovolaemia actually protects against weaning oedema."},"L12_9":{"clue":"Quiet-breathing excursion below 1.0 cm (men) or 0.9 cm (women).","mech":"Diaphragmatic contraction moves the dome caudally; reduced excursion reflects weakness, paralysis or ventilator-induced diaphragm dysfunction.","confirm":"Use M-mode via the liver window in the subcostal area; measure excursion during quiet breathing, and consider thickening fraction during spontaneous breathing trials.","trap":"Deep-breathing (about 4.7/3.7 cm) and sniff limits do not apply to quiet breathing; excursion under ventilator support reflects the ventilator, not the diaphragm."},"L12_10":{"clue":"Integrated cardiac, lung and venous POCUS for acute dyspnoea.","mech":"Bedside imaging quickly separates oedema, pneumonia, effusion, pneumothorax and PE, so correct treatment starts earlier.","confirm":"Integrate focused cardiac, lung and DVT scans with clinical data; evidence shows faster diagnosis and appropriate treatment.","trap":"No mortality benefit has been proven, and POCUS does not replace CT in all patients; claiming harder outcomes overstates the evidence."},"L13_1":{"clue":"The descending aorta is the landmark separating pericardial from left pleural fluid on PLAX.","mech":"The pericardial reflection lies anterior to the descending aorta; pleural fluid accumulates posterior and lateral to it.","confirm":"In PLAX locate the descending thoracic aorta behind the LA; fluid anterior to it is pericardial, fluid posterior to it is pleural. Confirm in subcostal or apical views.","trap":"Assuming all posterior fluid is pericardial overcalls effusions; echogenicity does not distinguish the two."},"L13_2":{"clue":"Echo-free space 15 mm at end-diastole.","mech":"Effusion size is graded by maximal end-diastolic separation: small below 10 mm, moderate 10-20 mm, large above 20 mm.","confirm":"Measure at end-diastole in multiple views; 15 mm corresponds to moderate (about 100-500 mL).","trap":"Measuring in systole overestimates; size does not define tamponade, which depends on rate of accumulation and pressure."},"L13_3":{"clue":"RV free-wall inversion in early diastole.","mech":"When pericardial pressure exceeds RV diastolic pressure, the thin RV wall collapses; RV pressure is higher than RA, so its collapse indicates higher pericardial pressure.","confirm":"Use M-mode in PLAX or subcostal to time RV collapse to early diastole; RA collapse lasting more than one-third of the cycle increases specificity.","trap":"RA collapse is more sensitive but less specific; Doppler variation and effusion size alone are not specific."},"L13_4":{"clue":"Respiratory variation of mitral E at least 30% and tricuspid E at least 60%.","mech":"Fixed pericardial volume causes ventricular interdependence: inspiration increases RV filling and decreases LV filling, exaggerating normal variation.","confirm":"Record PW mitral and tricuspid inflow with a respirometer; variation = (max - min)/max. Interpret with clinical signs.","trap":"Variation is blunted by mechanical ventilation and AF; COPD and RV failure cause false positives."},"L13_5":{"clue":"Plethoric IVC in tamponade.","mech":"Raised pericardial pressure elevates RA pressure, distending the IVC and reducing collapse in most cases.","confirm":"Assess IVC size and collapse; a normal collapsing IVC makes tamponade unlikely except in low-pressure tamponade.","trap":"A plethoric IVC is nonspecific (RV failure, TR, ventilation); its absence does not exclude low-pressure tamponade."},"L13_6":{"clue":"Pulmonary hypertension or RVH prevents RV collapse.","mech":"High RV pressure or thick walls resist compression; the left heart may collapse first.","confirm":"Look for LA or LV collapse, Doppler variation and IVC in RVH or PH; integrate clinical signs.","trap":"Assuming no RV collapse means no tamponade misses atypical tamponade in RVH, PH or localised effusions."},"L13_7":{"clue":"Hepatic vein flow with systolic forward dominance, loss of diastolic forward flow and expiratory diastolic reversal.","mech":"Total heart volume is fixed, so venous return can only occur when the ventricles eject (systole); in diastole there is no room, abolishing the y descent, worst in expiration when right filling falls.","confirm":"Record PW 1-2 cm into a hepatic vein from subcostal with a respirometer trace; note systolic dominance and expiratory diastolic reversal.","trap":"Inspiratory diastolic reversal suggests restriction; expiratory reversal also occurs in constriction, so hepatic Doppler alone cannot separate constriction from tamponade."},"L13_8":{"clue":"Annulus reversus, respirophasic septal shift, mitral variation above 25%, expiratory hepatic reversal.","mech":"Constriction limits total filling but myocardium relaxes normally; respiratory changes transmit to the ventricles, creating interdependence.","confirm":"Compare medial and lateral e'; look for septal bounce and Doppler variation; confirm with CT/MRI or catheterisation.","trap":"Restrictive cardiomyopathy shows low e', no respiratory variation and inspiratory hepatic reversal."},"L13_9":{"clue":"Haemopericardium from type A dissection or free-wall rupture.","mech":"Pericardial pressure may be temporarily tamponading the bleed; draining it raises systemic pressure and can extend the tear or reopen the rupture, causing rapid fatal re-accumulation.","confirm":"Look for a dissection flap, dilated root, AR or a regional wall defect; mobilise surgery. Only limited controlled drainage as a bridge if arrest is imminent.","trap":"Malignant and post-surgical effusions are routinely drained percutaneously; para-apical access is a technique, not a contraindication."},"L13_10":{"clue":"Apparently dilated RV in PLAX, RV at the centre of the apical image (teardrop heart), leftward shift.","mech":"Without the left pericardium the heart rotates and shifts into the left chest, so standard planes cut the RV more obliquely and it looks enlarged; the heart is hypermobile.","confirm":"Correlate with CXR (leftward shift, interposed lung) and ECG; confirm with CT or MRI showing absent pericardium.","trap":"Calling RV dilation from pressure or volume overload triggers unnecessary workup; check RV wall thickness, septal shape and pressures, which are normal."},"L14_1":{"clue":"Undifferentiated shock: first identify what will kill the patient in minutes.","mech":"Acute LV failure, tamponade and acute cor pulmonale are directly treatable causes of low output; finding them changes immediate management.","confirm":"Use focused views (PLAX, PSAX, A4C, subcostal, IVC) for LV function, effusion with chamber collapse and RV dilation, then classify the shock.","trap":"Diastolic grading, LV mass and Simpson EF are comprehensive measures that delay life-saving decisions."},"L14_2":{"clue":"Both obstructive, but tamponade has effusion with collapsing right chambers, whereas ACP has a dilated RV without effusion.","mech":"Tamponade compresses the heart from outside, reducing chamber size; ACP from raised afterload dilates the RV and shifts the septum.","confirm":"Subcostal and A4C views: look for effusion with RA/RV collapse versus RV/LV ratio above 1 with septal flattening (D-sign).","trap":"IVC is plethoric in both, so it cannot differentiate; treatments are opposite (drainage versus RV afterload reduction or lysis)."},"L14_3":{"clue":"Small IVC despite high CVP.","mech":"Raised intra-abdominal pressure compresses the intra-abdominal IVC externally, so it looks small even when RA pressure is high.","confirm":"Measure bladder pressure; integrate IVC with RA size, hepatic vein Doppler and clinical signs of abdominal compartment syndrome.","trap":"Severe TR, RV infarction and tamponade raise RAP and distend the IVC, the opposite pattern."},"L14_4":{"clue":"Low e' and high E/e' in cardiogenic shock.","mech":"Low e' reflects impaired relaxation; high E/e' reflects raised LA pressure, markers of severe myocardial dysfunction and congestion.","confirm":"Measure septal and lateral e' and E; average E/e' above 14 indicates high filling pressure and adverse prognosis.","trap":"High e' and low E/e' indicate preserved relaxation and low filling pressure, which are reassuring, not adverse."},"L14_5":{"clue":"Acute severe MR from flail leaflet or papillary muscle rupture.","mech":"Sudden massive regurgitation into a non-compliant LA causes acute pulmonary oedema and forward output falls, producing cardiogenic shock.","confirm":"Look for a flail segment or mobile papillary head, wide eccentric colour jet and hyperdynamic LV; confirm with TEE and urgent surgery referral.","trap":"In acute MR the colour jet may be brief and underestimated because LA and LV pressures equalise; mild AR, trace TR and MAC do not cause shock."},"L14_6":{"clue":"Dynamic LVOT obstruction occurs beyond HCM.","mech":"A hyperdynamic underfilled LV with a narrow outflow (basal septal bulge, Takotsubo basal hyperkinesis) drags the mitral leaflet into the LVOT by Venturi and drag forces (SAM).","confirm":"Look for SAM, a late-peaking dagger-shaped CW signal and mid-systolic aortic valve closure; check response to fluid and stopping inotropes.","trap":"Assuming obstruction only in HCM misses it in sepsis, hypovolaemia and post-op patients on inotropes; DCM and severe AR enlarge the cavity and prevent it."},"L14_7":{"clue":"Prone ARDS patient on PEEP 20 with new shock, dilated RV and septal flattening.","mech":"High PEEP and driving pressure compress alveolar vessels and raise PVR, and hypoxaemia and hypercapnia cause vasoconstriction, causing RV afterload failure.","confirm":"Measure RV/LV ratio, septal motion and TR velocity; reduce driving pressure and PEEP if overdistended, correct CO2, consider inhaled vasodilators.","trap":"Hypovolaemia gives a small RV; tamponade requires effusion; LVOT obstruction requires a hyperdynamic small LV."},"L14_8":{"clue":"Extra-cardiac components of shock protocols.","mech":"Shock can arise from pump (heart), tank (volume and leaks) or pipes (aorta and veins); each needs targeted views.","confirm":"RUSH or E-FAST: lungs (pneumothorax, B-lines, effusion), FAST views for free fluid, aorta (AAA, dissection) and femoral/popliteal DVT.","trap":"Restricting to cardiac or renal views misses haemorrhage, pneumothorax, AAA and DVT."},"L14_9":{"clue":"Circumferential apical akinesis beyond one coronary territory with basal hyperkinesis.","mech":"Catecholamine-mediated stunning involves all apical segments in a non-coronary pattern; LAD occlusion normally spares some apical segments supplied by other arteries.","confirm":"Map all apical segments in A4C, A2C and A3C; check for LVOT obstruction; confirm with angiography.","trap":"A wrap-around LAD can mimic Takotsubo; anteroseptal akinesis sparing the apex or isolated inferior/lateral akinesis suggests coronary infarction."},"L14_10":{"clue":"Tamponade is a clinical and haemodynamic diagnosis.","mech":"Echo shows raised pericardial pressure (chamber collapse, Doppler variation), but the decision to drain depends on haemodynamic compromise and clinical context.","confirm":"Integrate hypotension, tachycardia, pulsus paradoxus and elevated JVP with echo signs; drain when there is compromise.","trap":"Echo signs alone (effusion size, RA collapse) are not sufficient; draining stable patients for echo signs alone exposes them to complications."},"L15_1":{"clue":"Electrical activity with coordinated cardiac motion but no palpable pulse.","mech":"Pseudo-PEA has residual contractility and some forward flow, insufficient to generate a palpable pulse; causes are often reversible.","confirm":"Use echo during the pulse check to identify motion; search for tamponade, PE, hypovolaemia and severe LV dysfunction.","trap":"True PEA has no cardiac motion; confusing them misses reversible causes and mislabels prognosis."},"L15_2":{"clue":"Any visible cardiac activity during arrest, linked to survival to discharge.","mech":"Retained myocardial motion means viable myocardium that may respond to treatment; standstill means very little chance of ROSC.","confirm":"Look for motion on a clip recorded during the 10 s pause and review it during compressions; cardiac activity carries an OR of about 5.7 for survival to discharge.","trap":"OR 3.6 is the figure for survival to admission. Standstill predicts poor outcome but is not a stand-alone reason to stop; integrate it with the clinical picture."},"L15_3":{"clue":"The time limit for a combined pulse check and ultrasound look.","mech":"Coronary perfusion pressure falls quickly when compressions stop and takes several compressions to recover, so each long pause reduces the chance of ROSC.","confirm":"Leader counts down aloud, record a clip during a pause of 10 s or less, then interpret it while compressions continue.","trap":"Allowing 20-30 s to get a perfect view is the classic error; studies show POCUS pauses often exceed the limit unless actively controlled."},"L15_4":{"clue":"No usable view at the 10 s limit.","mech":"Compressions take priority over imaging; another window at the next check costs nothing, whereas extending the pause lowers coronary perfusion.","confirm":"Resume compressions immediately; pre-position for a parasternal or apical view, or use TEE for continuous imaging.","trap":"Scanning longer to get a view, or giving empirical fluid or lytics without a diagnosis, trades CPR quality for uncertain gain."},"L15_5":{"clue":"Monitor shows asystole but TEE in the series showed fibrillating myocardium in some.","mech":"Fine VF produces low-amplitude ECG signals that look like asystole, but the myocardium visibly quivers on ultrasound, so the rhythm is shockable.","confirm":"On TEE look for disorganised, rapid, low-amplitude myocardial fibrillation; if seen, defibrillate. In this series 4 of 33 (12%) had fine VF.","trap":"Assuming monitor asystole is true asystole withholds defibrillation; overestimating (50-80%) misreads the series."},"L15_6":{"clue":"TEE shows the zone of maximal compression over the aortic root or LVOT.","mech":"Compressing the outflow tract narrows it during the compression phase, impeding ejection; compressing the LV body squeezes blood forward through an open outflow.","confirm":"On ME LAX, watch which structure is compressed; move hand position toward the LV until the LV cavity is compressed and the outflow opens.","trap":"Assuming any compression is good compression ignores outflow obstruction found in about half of patients in TEE series."},"L15_7":{"clue":"Dilated hypokinetic RV with an underfilled LV in a patient with organised electrical activity.","mech":"Massive PE abruptly raises RV afterload; the RV dilates and fails, LV preload falls via series and septal interdependence, collapsing output.","confirm":"Look for RV/LV ratio above 1, septal flattening, clot in transit and leg DVT; consider thrombolysis in the arrest setting.","trap":"RV dilation also occurs after prolonged CPR from any cause; tamponade shows effusion with collapse, hypovolaemia small ventricles, and pump failure a dilated LV."},"L15_8":{"clue":"New regional wall motion abnormality after a VT/VF arrest.","mech":"A regional rather than global pattern after a shockable arrest suggests an acute coronary occlusion caused the arrest.","confirm":"Correlate with ECG, map the RWMA to a coronary territory and discuss urgent coronary angiography with cardiology.","trap":"Post-arrest global stunning is common and resolves; thrombolysis is not indicated for RWMA alone, and pericardiocentesis needs an effusion."},"L15_9":{"clue":"In-hospital arrest survival to discharge.","mech":"Survival depends on rhythm, time to CPR and defibrillation and comorbidity; in-hospital arrests are witnessed and monitored, giving better outcomes than out-of-hospital ones.","confirm":"Remember about 18% survive to discharge, most with good neurological function (Morrison 2013).","trap":"Survival of 50-75% describes ROSC or shockable witnessed arrests, not overall discharge; 2% is closer to unwitnessed out-of-hospital outcomes."},"L16_1":{"clue":"EF cut-off for preserved function in the lecture algorithm.","mech":"An EF at least 50% means systolic function is preserved, so heart failure is driven by diastolic dysfunction, valves, pericardium or other causes.","confirm":"Measure EF by biplane disks; if at least 50%, assess LV volumes, diastolic function and etiologic features (RWMA, wall thickness, valves, PA pressure).","trap":"40% is the HFrEF upper cut-off; 41-49% is mildly reduced. 35% is a device-therapy threshold, not a classification boundary."},"L16_2":{"clue":"High E/A, short DT, high E/e', large LA and raised PASP together.","mech":"Elevated LA pressure drives a high early E and a stiff LV causes rapid deceleration; chronic high pressure enlarges the LA and raises PASP.","confirm":"Apply E/A above 1.5, E/e' above 15, DT below 160 ms and LA enlargement with TR velocity to grade filling pressures.","trap":"E/A below 0.8 with long DT is impaired relaxation with normal LAP; normal LA and TR below 2.5 m/s argue against high filling pressure."},"L16_3":{"clue":"SV 26 mL, HR 101 and BSA 1.51 m2.","mech":"Cardiac index normalises output to body size; low CI indicates inadequate perfusion.","confirm":"CO = 26 mL x 101 = 2.63 L/min; CI = 2.63/1.51 = about 1.74 L/min/m2 (low). SV = pi x (1.1 cm)2 x VTI.","trap":"Forgetting to divide by BSA gives 2.6; using LVOT diameter instead of radius in the area formula inflates SV."},"L16_4":{"clue":"Mayo criteria for Takotsubo.","mech":"Catecholamine-mediated myocardial stunning causes transient wall motion abnormalities beyond a single coronary territory, without plaque rupture.","confirm":"Look for apical ballooning or variant patterns, rule out obstructive CAD with angiography, and document recovery on follow-up echo.","trap":"A fixed single-territory RWMA suggests infarction; EF below 20% is not a criterion; pheochromocytoma must be excluded, but it is not the first criterion."},"L16_5":{"clue":"Asymmetric septal thickening (about 19 mm versus 12 mm) with very low e' and high E/e'.","mech":"HCM is a primary myocardial disease with myocyte disarray, producing asymmetric hypertrophy and markedly impaired relaxation; hypertensive LVH is concentric and milder.","confirm":"Measure septal and posterior wall thickness, septal e' and E/e'; look for SAM and LVOT gradient.","trap":"Mild concentric thickening suggests hypertension; normal e' argues against HCM; dilated LV suggests other cardiomyopathies."},"L16_6":{"clue":"Thick LV and RV walls with sparkling, biatrial enlargement, restrictive filling, apical-sparing strain.","mech":"Amyloid fibrils infiltrate the myocardium, increasing thickness without hypertrophy and causing restrictive physiology.","confirm":"Assess strain (apical sparing), diastolic function and look for low-voltage ECG; confirm with technetium scan, serum free light chains or biopsy.","trap":"Thin walls with dilation suggest DCM; isolated septal bulge is common in the elderly; normal diastolic function argues against amyloid."},"L16_7":{"clue":"Thick LV walls, binary endocardial appearance and low alpha-galactosidase A in renal failure.","mech":"Fabry is an X-linked lysosomal storage disease; glycosphingolipid accumulates in myocardium, causing hypertrophy and a two-layered endocardium.","confirm":"Confirm low enzyme activity (or GLA gene mutation), look for inferolateral LGE on CMR; assess renal and neurological involvement.","trap":"Amyloid also thickens walls but is not linked to low alpha-galactosidase; the binary sign is neither sensitive nor specific alone."},"L16_8":{"clue":"Mechanical MI complications seen on bedside echo.","mech":"Necrotic myocardium can rupture (free wall, septum, papillary muscle) or remodel, causing tethering and ischaemic MR.","confirm":"Use colour Doppler for VSD and MR, look for effusion with tamponade, and identify mobile papillary heads.","trap":"Coronary dissection and Dressler syndrome are not primary echo diagnoses; LV thrombus is only one of several complications."},"L16_9":{"clue":"Preferred method for LV volumes and EF in heart failure.","mech":"Volumetric methods (biplane disks or 3D) account for LV shape distortion and regional wall motion, improving accuracy.","confirm":"Use biplane disks or 3D volumes; add GLS for subclinical dysfunction.","trap":"Linear Teichholz and EPSS assume symmetric geometry; visual estimates alone are less reproducible."},"L16_10":{"clue":"Stages of eosinophilic myocarditis.","mech":"Eosinophil infiltration causes necrosis, endocardial damage, thrombus formation and fibrosis, leading to restrictive physiology.","confirm":"Look for apical thrombus over abnormal myocardium, then restrictive filling and posterior leaflet tethering; confirm with eosinophil count and CMR.","trap":"Hypertrophy, calcification or fatty infiltration are unrelated stages."},"B9_3":{"clue":"Regional pattern: basal and mid RV free wall hypokinetic while the RV apex still contracts.","mech":"Acute afterload rise strains the basal and mid free wall most; the apex may be tethered to a hyperdynamic LV, so apical motion is relatively preserved.","confirm":"In an RV-focused apical 4-chamber view, watch free wall excursion segment by segment and pair it with RV size, septal shape and TAPSE.","trap":"Do not confuse it with the shared apex sign, which reflects RV dilation; and McConnell is not specific to PE, since RV infarction can mimic it."},"B9_4":{"clue":"Basal RV diameter of 44 mm in an RV-focused apical 4-chamber view exceeds the 41 mm threshold.","mech":"The RV dilates mainly at its base and mid cavity in response to volume or pressure load, so basal width is the most sensitive linear index.","confirm":"Measure at end-diastole in an RV-focused view: basal >41 mm, mid >35 mm, longitudinal >83 mm define dilation.","trap":"The other values sit below their cutoffs (mid 35, longitudinal 83, distal RVOT 27 mm); a foreshortened standard A4C also underestimates RV size."},"B9_5":{"clue":"RV free wall 7 mm at end-diastole in the subcostal view, above the 5 mm upper limit.","mech":"Hypertrophy needs weeks to months of sustained pressure load; an acutely obstructed RV dilates but cannot thicken within days.","confirm":"Measure free wall thickness in subcostal view at end-diastole excluding trabeculae and epicardial fat; look for high PASP (TR velocity) suggesting chronic pulmonary hypertension.","trap":"Assuming any RV strain pattern in suspected PE is acute; a thick RV with very high PASP points to chronic or acute-on-chronic disease such as CTEPH."},"B9_6":{"clue":"Septal flattening in both systole and diastole on parasternal short axis, giving a D-shaped LV throughout the cycle.","mech":"With pressure overload RV pressure approaches or exceeds LV pressure even in systole, so the septum is pushed leftward all cycle long.","confirm":"Time the flattening with ECG or M-mode and calculate eccentricity index; estimate PASP from TR velocity plus RA pressure.","trap":"Volume overload flattens the septum mainly in diastole with normal systolic curvature; diastolic-only flattening points away from pure pressure load."},"B9_7":{"clue":"The question asks about ruling out PE with echocardiography.","mech":"Many PEs, especially small or peripheral clots, do not raise RV afterload enough to cause visible RV dilation or dysfunction, so a normal echo is common.","confirm":"Use CT pulmonary angiography or compression ultrasound and pretest probability to exclude PE; reserve echo for unstable patients to detect RV overload.","trap":"Assuming a normal RV excludes PE; echo is a poor rule-out test and is most useful for diagnosis and risk stratification in shock."},"B9_8":{"clue":"Wedge-shaped, homogeneous, hypoechoic pleural-based lesion in the dorsal lower lobe plus a small effusion.","mech":"Arterial occlusion causes hemorrhagic infarction of the peripheral lung, which appears as a solid, airless, pleural-based wedge without air bronchograms.","confirm":"Look for multiple similar lesions, absent air bronchograms and absent internal flow, then perform compression ultrasound of the legs for DVT.","trap":"Calling it pneumonia: consolidation from pneumonia usually shows dynamic air bronchograms and irregular margins, not a homogeneous wedge."},"B9_9":{"clue":"The vein fails to collapse completely under pressure sufficient to deform the adjacent artery.","mech":"A normal vein has low intraluminal pressure and collapses easily; thrombus fills the lumen and prevents apposition of the walls.","confirm":"Compress the common femoral and popliteal veins in transverse view, applying enough pressure to deform the artery, and document full or incomplete wall apposition.","trap":"Waiting to see echogenic thrombus: fresh thrombus can be anechoic, so incompressibility, not visible clot, defines a positive study."},"B9_10":{"clue":"Lateral-to-medial order in the femoral triangle: nerve, artery, vein.","mech":"Anatomy follows the NAVY mnemonic (Nerve, Artery, Vein, Y-fronts toward the midline), with the vein most medial.","confirm":"Identify the pulsatile, non-compressible artery with color Doppler; the vein lies medial and collapses with pressure.","trap":"Mistaking the artery for a thrombosed vein because both are non-compressible; in the popliteal fossa the vein is superficial to the artery."},"B9_11":{"clue":"High-frequency linear probe, compressions about every 1 cm, reverse Trendelenburg.","mech":"A 5-10 MHz probe gives the resolution needed for superficial veins, and head-up tilt distends leg veins so compressibility is easier to judge.","confirm":"Compress serially from the common femoral vein through the femoral and deep femoral bifurcation and in the popliteal fossa past the crease.","trap":"Trendelenburg empties leg veins; single-point compression skips segments; and Doppler augmentation is not the primary criterion."},"B9_12":{"clue":"Color Doppler is used to identify vessels and to separate them from nodes or nerves.","mech":"Compression is the diagnostic criterion; color flow simply confirms that a tubular structure is a vessel rather than a solid structure.","confirm":"Apply color to identify the artery and vein, then rely on compression for diagnosis; image iliac veins or use CT for suspected pelvic DVT.","trap":"Believing the 2-point exam sees pelvic veins or that ACEP mandates augmentation; an occlusive thrombus blunts, not increases, augmented flow."},"B10_1":{"clue":"Circumferential end-diastolic echo-free space of 22 mm.","mech":"Size is graded by the maximal end-diastolic separation of pericardial layers: moderate 10-20 mm, large over 20 mm, very large over 25 mm.","confirm":"Measure in end-diastole in more than one view and pair size with chamber collapse, IVC and Doppler findings for hemodynamic effect.","trap":"Equating size with tamponade; hemodynamic impact depends on rate of accumulation and pericardial compliance, not size alone."},"B10_2":{"clue":"In PLAX, fluid tracks anterior to the descending thoracic aorta, between it and the LA.","mech":"The pericardial reflection lies anterior to the descending aorta, whereas the left pleural space extends posterior to it.","confirm":"Locate the descending aorta behind the LA in PLAX; fluid between aorta and heart is pericardial, fluid behind it is pleural.","trap":"An anterior-only echo space that moves with the heart and is brighter than myocardium is usually epicardial fat, not effusion."},"B10_3":{"clue":"Small to moderate effusion causing shock minutes after a lead procedure.","mech":"The pericardial pressure-volume curve is steep; without time to stretch, a small volume sharply raises intrapericardial pressure.","confirm":"Look for RA and RV diastolic collapse and a plethoric IVC; urgent pericardiocentesis with hemodynamic response confirms tamponade.","trap":"Dismissing tamponade because the effusion is small; rate of accumulation, not volume, determines the pressure rise."},"B10_4":{"clue":"RA collapse beginning in late diastole or early systole, more specific if it lasts over 30% of the cycle.","mech":"The thin-walled, low-pressure RA collapses when its pressure is lowest during atrial relaxation; prolonged collapse means intrapericardial pressure exceeds RA pressure for longer.","confirm":"Use apical 4-chamber or subcostal views with M-mode or frame-by-frame review to time collapse relative to the ECG.","trap":"RA collapse is typically the first and most sensitive sign; brief collapse alone is nonspecific, and it is not best seen in PLAX."},"B10_5":{"clue":"RV free wall indentation just after the T wave, that is in early diastole.","mech":"In early diastole RV pressure is at its lowest while intrapericardial pressure is high, so the compliant RV free wall buckles inward.","confirm":"Place M-mode through the RV free wall in PLAX or subcostal view and time inward motion against ECG and mitral opening.","trap":"Calling it RA collapse or normal: RA collapse is atrial and late diastolic; RV early diastolic collapse is less sensitive but more specific."},"B10_6":{"clue":"Plethoric IVC with hepatic vein diastolic flow blunted or reversed in expiration.","mech":"Tamponade fixes total cardiac volume, so venous return occurs mainly in systole; in expiration right heart filling falls further, producing diastolic flow reversal.","confirm":"Record hepatic vein PW Doppler with a respirometer trace, noting expiratory diastolic reversal and systolic predominance.","trap":"IVC plethora alone is sensitive but nonspecific; inspiratory reversal is more typical of constriction or pulmonary hypertension, not tamponade."},"B10_7":{"clue":"Mitral E 100 cm/s in expiration and 62 cm/s in inspiration.","mech":"In tamponade, inspiratory rise in right filling shifts the septum left within a fixed pericardial volume, reducing LV inflow.","confirm":"Variation = (Exp minus Insp) / Exp = (100 minus 62)/100 = 38%, above the 30% mitral threshold.","trap":"Applying the 60% tricuspid threshold to mitral inflow, or dividing by the inspiratory value; and inflow variation alone is never diagnostic."},"B10_8":{"clue":"Positive pressure ventilation with no inflow variation or collapse, but a dilated IVC and a small RV cavity.","mech":"PPV reverses and blunts normal respiratory loading, masking classic signs; high RA pressure with a small RV implies external compression of the RV.","confirm":"Pair the dilated IVC and small RV with clinical shock and consider TEE for loculated collection; drainage with hemodynamic improvement confirms.","trap":"Excluding tamponade because Doppler variation and collapse are absent; these signs are unreliable under mechanical ventilation."},"B10_9":{"clue":"RA and RV diastolic collapse with a small, collapsible IVC in a hemorrhaging patient.","mech":"Hypovolemia lowers intracardiac pressures, so a modest intrapericardial pressure is enough to collapse chambers without venous congestion.","confirm":"Correlate collapse with shock and consider cautious volume plus pericardiocentesis; watch for improvement after drainage.","trap":"Using a small IVC to exclude tamponade; IVC plethora is absent in low-pressure tamponade."},"B10_10":{"clue":"Chronic pulmonary hypertension with RV hypertrophy and a large effusion with hypotension.","mech":"A thick, high-pressure RV resists external compression, so the lower-pressure LV or left atrium becomes the chamber that collapses.","confirm":"Look for LV or LA diastolic collapse and absent RV collapse; tricuspid inflow variation may also be missing.","trap":"Assuming pulmonary hypertension prevents tamponade or that the RV must collapse; it only changes which chamber yields."},"B10_11":{"clue":"Post-cardiac surgery shock with high right pressures and no effusion seen on TTE.","mech":"Postoperative bleeding often forms a loculated, posterior clot compressing atria or ventricles regionally, in windows TTE images poorly.","confirm":"Perform urgent TEE to look for localized hematoma compressing the RA, LA or ventricles, and involve surgeons for reexploration.","trap":"Trusting a negative TTE or pulsus paradoxus; regional tamponade often lacks classic signs and equalization of pressures."},"B10_12":{"clue":"Interim bridge: fluid helps only some patients and norepinephrine raises MAP.","mech":"Volume can raise filling pressure above pericardial pressure in hypovolemic patients, but in others it increases pericardial pressure further and worsens filling.","confirm":"Give small boluses only with monitoring of BP and cardiac output, use norepinephrine for MAP, and proceed promptly to drainage.","trap":"Routine fluid for all, or the belief that low RA pressure predicts benefit; definitive treatment is drainage, not a drug bridge."},"B10_13":{"clue":"New effusion on VA ECMO with failed weaning and equivocal collapse.","mech":"ECMO drains the right heart and supports flow, masking collapse and pressure changes; the effusion still restricts native cardiac filling when flow is reduced.","confirm":"Look for loss of pulsatility, regional compression on TEE and response to drainage or clot evacuation.","trap":"Labelling it hypovolemia or primary RV failure because classic tamponade signs are absent; on ECMO they are unreliable."},"B10_14":{"clue":"Acute RV dilation and shock hours after surgical drainage in a patient with previously normal function.","mech":"Pericardial decompression syndrome: sudden loss of external constraint causes abrupt venous return and ventricular mismatch, producing paradoxical failure.","confirm":"Repeat echo for new RV, LV or biventricular dysfunction and exclude reaccumulation or bleeding; support with inotropes and mechanical support if needed.","trap":"It can occur up to 48 hours later, usually with normal prior function, and is not caused by fluid reaccumulation."},"B11_1":{"clue":"Mitral E 110 cm/s with septal e' 7 cm/s.","mech":"E rises with LA pressure while e' reflects relaxation and is relatively load-independent, so the ratio tracks filling pressure.","confirm":"Septal E/e' = 110/7 = about 16, above the septal cutoff of 15; corroborate with LA size and TR velocity if available.","trap":"Using the average cutoff of 14 to call it indeterminate, or believing a TR jet is needed before E/e' can be interpreted."},"B11_2":{"clue":"Normal lower limits: septal e' at least 7 cm/s and lateral e' at least 10 cm/s.","mech":"The lateral annulus moves faster because it is less tethered by the stiffer septum and its fibres move more freely.","confirm":"Place the TDI sample at the septal and lateral annulus in apical 4-chamber, align with motion and measure early diastolic peak.","trap":"Reversing the values: septal e' is normally lower than lateral, not higher."},"B11_3":{"clue":"E/A 0.6 with low septal e' and E/e' 12 and a normal LA.","mech":"Slow relaxation reduces early filling so atrial contraction contributes more, but LA pressure is not yet high enough to restore E.","confirm":"Check the four criteria: E/e' not over 14, normal LA volume and TR velocity under 2.8 m/s support normal LAP.","trap":"Calling it pseudonormal or restrictive; those require E/A above 0.8 with elevated filling pressure markers."},"B11_4":{"clue":"Ambiguous E/A of 1.2; preload reduction to unmask impaired relaxation.","mech":"Valsalva strain lowers venous return and LAP; in pseudonormal filling E falls and the pattern reverts toward impaired relaxation.","confirm":"Record mitral inflow during strain phase; a 50% or greater fall in E/A indicates elevated filling pressure.","trap":"Fluid or PLR increase preload rather than reduce it, and PEEP effects on deceleration time are not a validated discriminator."},"B11_5":{"clue":"E/A 2.4, low e', E/e' 18 and very short deceleration time.","mech":"A stiff LV with high LAP fills rapidly in early diastole and pressures equalize quickly, so E is tall and decelerates fast.","confirm":"Confirm E/A at least 2 with short DT under 160 ms, high E/e' and dilated LA, indicating grade III.","trap":"Calling it pseudonormal; that pattern has E/A between 0.8 and 2 and needs filling pressure markers to identify."},"B11_6":{"clue":"TR velocity 3.3 m/s in a patient with ARDS.","mech":"TR velocity reflects PA systolic pressure, which mirrors LAP only if pulmonary vascular resistance is normal; ARDS raises PVR independently.","confirm":"Use E/e', LA size, pulmonary vein flow and lateral E/e' instead; compare with RV size and function to judge pulmonary vascular disease.","trap":"Assuming TR velocity is load-independent or valid only in AF or subcostal views; its limit is pulmonary vascular disease."},"B11_7":{"clue":"LAVI cutoff of 34 mL/m2, which reflects chronic remodeling.","mech":"LA enlargement develops over time from sustained high pressure, so it is a marker of chronicity rather than acute filling pressure.","confirm":"Measure by biplane method of discs at end-systole, excluding appendage and pulmonary veins, and index to BSA.","trap":"Assuming LAVI tracks acute changes; acute rises may not dilate the LA and ventilation alters atrial size."},"B11_8":{"clue":"AF with variable RR intervals; E/e' remains usable with matched beats.","mech":"E and e' both vary with cycle length; taking them from beats with similar preceding intervals keeps the ratio internally consistent.","confirm":"Measure E and e' from cycles with comparable preceding RR intervals or average about 10 beats.","trap":"There is no A wave in AF, so E/A is invalid, and LAVI is confounded by AF itself."},"B11_9":{"clue":"Suspected constriction with high septal e' of 12 cm/s and low E/e'.","mech":"In constriction the pericardium restricts lateral motion, so longitudinal septal motion is preserved or exaggerated, making e' high despite high LAP (annulus reversus).","confirm":"Compare septal and lateral e' (septal higher than lateral), look for septal bounce and respiratory variation in inflow and hepatic veins.","trap":"Reading low E/e' as normal LAP; E/e' assumptions also fail in MS, prosthetic MV, significant MR and regional WMAs."},"B11_10":{"clue":"PW gate at the mitral leaflet tips within the LV.","mech":"The highest inflow velocities are at the leaflet tips where the orifice is narrowest; more apical sampling records slower, dispersed flow.","confirm":"Use apical 4-chamber, 1-3 mm gate at the tips, angle under 20 degrees, average 3 beats at end-expiration.","trap":"Sampling at the annulus measures annular inflow, and tissue Doppler filters are for e' not E."},"B11_11":{"clue":"High E/e' used to reject fluid; the question distinguishes LAP from responsiveness.","mech":"Responsiveness depends on whether the ventricle operates on the steep part of its Frank-Starling curve, not on absolute filling pressure.","confirm":"Use a dynamic test such as PLR with VTI change or pulse pressure variation; use E/e' for fluid tolerance judgement.","trap":"Assuming high filling pressure means no response; static pressures, like PCWP, do not predict responsiveness."},"B11_12":{"clue":"Heart rate 135/min with fused mitral E and A waves.","mech":"Short diastole superimposes early and atrial filling; the fused peak still largely reflects the LA-LV gradient, so its ratio to e' tracks filling pressure.","confirm":"Take the fused peak as E and divide by e' (septal, lateral or average), interpreting against the usual cutoffs.","trap":"Declaring the study indeterminate or using deceleration time; E/A is lost with fusion but E/e' remains generally usable."},"B12_1":{"clue":"Small, hyperdynamic LV with near cavity obliteration and LVEDA 4.8 cm2/m2.","mech":"Low venous return reduces end-diastolic filling, so the LV cavity is small and contracts to near obliteration.","confirm":"Indexed LVEDA under 5.5 cm2/m2 supports hypovolemia; add IVC indices and a dynamic preload test such as PLR with VTI.","trap":"Confusing the indexed threshold (5.5 cm2/m2) with the absolute value (over 12 cm2 argues against hypovolemia), or labelling it LVOT obstruction without Doppler."},"B12_2":{"clue":"Passively ventilated patient: IVC distensibility index over 18%.","mech":"Under positive pressure the IVC distends with inspiration; large distension implies the vein sits on the compliant part of its curve, suggesting low volume.","confirm":"Distensibility = (Dmax minus Dmin)/Dmin, measured with M-mode 2 cm from the RA junction in a fully passive patient with tidal volume around 8 mL/kg.","trap":"Using collapsibility thresholds (50% or 75%), which apply to spontaneously breathing patients, not to ventilated ones."},"B12_3":{"clue":"FUSA starts by asking whether chronic cardiac disease is present.","mech":"Chronic dilation or hypertrophy alters baseline chamber sizes and filling pressures, confounding focused volume and pump assessment.","confirm":"Look for LV or RV dilation and hypertrophy, LA enlargement and valve disease before the steps of pump, volume, tamponade and lung.","trap":"Assuming the first step is tamponade or VTI as in other shock protocols; FUSA explicitly screens chronic disease first."},"B12_4":{"clue":"Dilated, apex-forming RV with RV/LV over 1 and septal flattening, but a thin 3 mm free wall.","mech":"The RV dilates acutely when afterload rises abruptly; hypertrophy needs weeks, so a thin wall points to an acute insult.","confirm":"Measure free wall thickness in subcostal end-diastole (5 mm or less is thin), check TR velocity and look for DVT or thrombus in transit.","trap":"Calling chronic pulmonary hypertension; that produces a thick RV wall and usually a higher PASP than an acutely failing RV can generate."},"B12_5":{"clue":"The pipes step of RUSH: aorta and deep veins.","mech":"Ruptured or obstructed vessels cause shock by hemorrhage (aortic rupture, dissection) or obstruction (DVT leading to PE).","confirm":"Image the aortic root in PLAX, suprasternal arch, abdominal aorta segments and compress femoral and popliteal veins.","trap":"IVC, lung and FAST belong to the tank step; pericardium and RV:LV belong to the pump step."},"B12_6":{"clue":"Developing tamponade: the earliest chamber to collapse.","mech":"The RA has the lowest pressure and thinnest wall; during atrial relaxation in systole, intrapericardial pressure exceeds it first.","confirm":"Time collapse on apical 4-chamber or subcostal view with ECG or M-mode; then look for RV diastolic collapse as severity increases.","trap":"RV diastolic collapse is more specific but appears later; LV and LA collapse are uncommon except in loculated or right-sided pressure overload states."},"B12_7":{"clue":"PLAX with depth enough to see the descending aorta: pericardial fluid lies anterior to it.","mech":"The posterior pericardial reflection sits between the heart and the descending aorta; the left pleural space extends behind the aorta.","confirm":"Increase depth to display the descending aorta and note whether fluid tapers in front of or extends behind it.","trap":"Assuming any posterior fluid is pericardial; fluid behind the aorta is pleural, and pericardial fluid does not usually extend behind the LA."},"B12_8":{"clue":"Post-cardiotomy shock with an apparently compressed LV and no effusion on TTE.","mech":"Loculated clot behind the heart can compress the left chambers selectively without visible circumferential fluid on limited postoperative windows.","confirm":"Perform TEE to image posterior structures and look for clot compressing atria or ventricles; inform the surgeon.","trap":"Excluding tamponade because TTE shows no fluid or the right heart is not compressed; regional tamponade can spare the right side."},"B12_9":{"clue":"SV rises from 50 to 59 mL after fluid challenge.","mech":"A rise in SV with preload means the ventricle is on the ascending part of the Frank-Starling curve.","confirm":"Percent change = (59 minus 50)/50 = 18%, above the 15% threshold for fluid responsiveness.","trap":"Applying the 20% contractile reserve threshold, which belongs to inotrope testing, not fluid challenges."},"B12_10":{"clue":"Dynamic LVOT obstruction or severe AR invalidates LVOT VTI as an SV surrogate.","mech":"Obstruction distorts flow and velocity profile in the LVOT, and severe AR adds regurgitant volume so forward flow differs from total LVOT flow.","confirm":"Check for SAM, aliasing or late-peaking LVOT signal, and grade AR before relying on VTI; use an alternative site if invalid.","trap":"Moderate MR, PEEP and normal heart rates do not invalidate LVOT VTI; it still measures forward SV."},"B12_11":{"clue":"Small, hyperdynamic LV with increased SV, normal LVEDA and normal IVC.","mech":"Low afterload lets the LV empty more completely, yielding high SV with normal filling, as in vasodilatory states.","confirm":"Confirm high VTI and cardiac output with normal LVEDA and IVC; exclude other causes since vasodilatory shock is diagnosed by exclusion.","trap":"A small LV with low SV suggests underfilling or obstruction; here SV is increased, which points to low afterload rather than hypovolemia."},"B12_12":{"clue":"LV hypertrophy, hypotension on dobutamine and a late-peaking, dagger-shaped LVOT signal.","mech":"Inotropy and hypovolemia shrink the hypertrophied LV cavity so the outflow narrows progressively in systole, producing a late-peaking gradient.","confirm":"Walk PW from apex to LVOT to localise aliasing, look for SAM, and measure peak gradient with CW; stop inotropes and give volume.","trap":"Confusing with MR: MR is symmetric and earlier peaking; AR is diastolic and directed toward the LV."},"B13_1":{"clue":"IVC collapsed throughout the respiratory cycle in a ventilated patient despite RV overload and effusion.","mech":"Under positive pressure a persistently collapsed IVC means low right atrial pressure, so preload is insufficient to tolerate higher intrathoracic pressure.","confirm":"Give a fluid bolus and reassess IVC, VTI and blood pressure before raising PEEP or recruiting.","trap":"Diuresing for B lines or draining the effusion; low preload would make recruitment poorly tolerated and the effusion is not causing tamponade with low pressures."},"B13_2":{"clue":"Posterior zones in the 12-zone protocol.","mech":"Supine ventilated patients develop gravity-dependent posterior consolidation and loss of aeration that anterior zones miss.","confirm":"Scan anterior, lateral and posterior zones superior and inferior on each side to compute an aeration score.","trap":"Believing it is the only CT-validated protocol or the fastest for pneumothorax; no head-to-head comparison of protocols exists."},"B13_3":{"clue":"A 28-zone protocol for interstitial syndrome monitoring.","mech":"Counting B lines over many anterior and lateral sites quantifies extravascular lung water in heart failure.","confirm":"Count B lines in each zone and sum the total for serial comparison.","trap":"Pneumothorax screening in trauma uses an abbreviated anterior protocol, not 28 zones."},"B13_4":{"clue":"BLUE protocol PE accuracy: sensitivity 81% and specificity 99%.","mech":"In the protocol, PE is called when lungs show an A profile plus venous thrombosis, a combination that is very specific but misses PE without DVT.","confirm":"Combine A profile lungs with compression ultrasound; a positive DVT supports PE, while a negative does not exclude it.","trap":"Inverting the numbers; the protocol is far more specific than sensitive for PE because not all PE patients have visible DVT."},"B13_5":{"clue":"Absent lung sliding over an entire hemithorax with A lines but no lung point, after intubation.","mech":"With right mainstem intubation the left lung is not ventilated, so the pleura do not slide although they remain in contact.","confirm":"Look for a lung pulse, which proves pleural apposition, and check tube position by auscultation, CXR or tube depth.","trap":"Absent sliding alone is not pneumothorax; only a lung point is specific, and lung pulse excludes pneumothorax at that site."},"B13_6":{"clue":"Pericardial movement in the paracardiac area mimics a lung point.","mech":"Cardiac motion next to the pleura creates an alternating pattern that resembles the edge of a pneumothorax.","confirm":"Move away from the heart border and confirm the pattern changes with respiration, not with the cardiac cycle.","trap":"Assuming any transition is a lung point; diaphragm motion, adhesions or bulla edges can also mimic it, and complete pneumothorax has no lung point."},"B13_7":{"clue":"Dependent echogenic layer with an anechoic layer above in a trauma effusion.","mech":"With time, red cells sediment dependently while serum separates above, forming a hematocrit sign.","confirm":"Correlate with the timing of injury and hemoglobin trend; perform diagnostic thoracentesis or CT as needed.","trap":"Acute hemothorax is usually homogeneously echogenic; layering suggests an older bleed."},"B13_8":{"clue":"Completely anechoic pleural effusion.","mech":"Echogenicity depends on cells, protein and fibrin; many exudates remain anechoic, so absence of echoes cannot classify the fluid.","confirm":"Perform diagnostic thoracentesis and apply Light criteria to classify the fluid.","trap":"Assuming anechoic means transudate; transudates are usually anechoic but exudates frequently are too."},"B13_9":{"clue":"Multiple coalescent B lines in an intercostal space.","mech":"Coalescent B lines reflect thickened interlobular septa and alveolar flooding leaving little air, so aeration is severely reduced.","confirm":"Score the region as 2 in the aeration score and compare with consolidation (score 3) and well-spaced B lines (score 1).","trap":"Isolated B lines in dependent areas or the elderly may be normal; well-spaced B lines mean moderate, not severe, loss."},"B13_10":{"clue":"Vertical hyperechoic artifacts with subcutaneous emphysema after chest tube insertion.","mech":"Air in subcutaneous tissue generates E lines that start above the pleural line and obscure deeper structures.","confirm":"Check whether the artifacts arise from the pleural line; true B lines start at the pleura and move with lung sliding.","trap":"Calling them B lines gives false interstitial syndrome and falsely excludes pneumothorax."},"B13_11":{"clue":"High pretest probability (PJP with sudden dyspnea) and LUS showing sliding in a semi-recumbent patient.","mech":"Free air rises to the least dependent area; when not fully supine, the apex rather than the anterior chest is least dependent and may be missed.","confirm":"Scan higher apical regions and obtain CXR or CT if clinical suspicion persists.","trap":"Overtrusting a negative LUS in high-risk patients; sliding excludes pneumothorax only at the area imaged."},"B13_12":{"clue":"Acute respiratory failure and pleuritic pain with normal LUS (A lines and sliding).","mech":"A normal aerated lung with sliding excludes pneumothorax, edema and consolidation, leaving vascular causes such as PE.","confirm":"Perform compression ultrasound of the legs and assess RV size, septal shape and TR velocity.","trap":"LV assessment is the next step for diffuse B lines, not for a normal A-line profile."},"B14_1":{"clue":"End-SBT LUS aeration score of 19.","mech":"Loss of aeration at the end of the SBT reflects derecruitment or edema that will worsen after extubation.","confirm":"Compare with thresholds: over 17 predicts failure, under 13 predicts success; optimize aeration and repeat.","trap":"Assuming passing a 1-hour SBT guarantees success; aeration loss predicts distress despite passing."},"B14_2":{"clue":"Coalescent B lines over more than half of the pleural line in a region.","mech":"Coalescent lines indicate severe interstitial and alveolar involvement with little residual air.","confirm":"Score 0 for A lines or 2 or fewer B lines, 1 for 3 or more spaced B lines, 2 for coalescent lines, 3 for consolidation.","trap":"Scoring it 3, which is reserved for tissue-like consolidation."},"B14_3":{"clue":"Twelve regions each scored 0 to 3.","mech":"Anterior, lateral and posterior areas, each superior and inferior, give 6 regions per hemithorax.","confirm":"Multiply 12 regions by 3 points to give a maximum score of 36.","trap":"Forgetting posterior zones or counting 6 regions total; the global score includes both hemithoraces."},"B14_4":{"clue":"Diaphragm thickness 2.0 mm at end-expiration and 2.3 mm at end-inspiration.","mech":"Thickening of the diaphragm reflects active muscle contraction; low thickening fraction indicates weak inspiratory effort.","confirm":"TF = (2.3 minus 2.0)/2.0 = 15%, below the 20% normal threshold.","trap":"Dividing by inspiratory thickness or interpreting absolute thickness alone; TF depends on the change from baseline."},"B14_5":{"clue":"Right hemidiaphragm excursion 0.8 cm with M-mode.","mech":"Excursion reflects caudal displacement during inspiration; values below 1 cm mean the diaphragm generates little volume.","confirm":"Use low-frequency probe in the subcostal view and measure excursion with M-mode along the movement axis; compare with 1.5-2.5 cm normal.","trap":"Assuming M-mode cannot be used or that 0.8 cm is mild; under 1 cm defines severe dysfunction."},"B14_6":{"clue":"Excursion measured during pressure support reflects both patient and ventilator activity.","mech":"Positive pressure passively displaces the diaphragm, so excursion does not purely represent muscle function.","confirm":"Measure during unassisted breathing (T-piece) or use thickening fraction, which reflects active contraction.","trap":"Thinking excursion correlates poorly with fluoroscopy; it correlates well, but ventilator assistance confounds it."},"B14_7":{"clue":"Lateral E/e' rising from 7 to 14 during an SBT with preserved LVEF.","mech":"Removing positive pressure increases venous return and LV afterload, raising LV filling pressure and causing weaning-induced edema.","confirm":"Repeat E/e', look for new B lines and rising LAP markers; diurese and reassess before the next SBT.","trap":"Labelling it diaphragm or lung failure; the cardiac Doppler change identifies a filling pressure mechanism."},"B14_8":{"clue":"Pseudonormal or restrictive mitral inflow in weaning patients.","mech":"High filling pressure at baseline worsens when intrathoracic pressure falls, precipitating pulmonary edema during weaning.","confirm":"Perform CCE after any weaning or extubation failure, measuring inflow pattern and E/e'.","trap":"Focusing on LVEF; reduced EF is present in fewer than 20% of weaning patients, while diastolic dysfunction is common."},"B14_9":{"clue":"Mitral prosthesis invalidates E/e'.","mech":"The prosthesis alters transmitral flow and annular motion, so E and e' no longer reflect filling pressure and relaxation.","confirm":"Use alternative indices such as pulmonary vein flow, LA size and clinical findings instead.","trap":"Mild tachycardia, mild TR and norepinephrine do not invalidate E/e'; MS, MAC and significant MR do."},"B14_10":{"clue":"Septal hypertrophy with hypotension, new murmur and MR from dynamic LVOT obstruction during SBT.","mech":"Adrenergic surge and tachycardia shrink the LV cavity, causing SAM, obstruction and MR; slowing the rate and increasing afterload reduce it.","confirm":"Use CW Doppler for a late-peaking LVOT gradient and color for posterior MR; give beta-blocker and phenylephrine and reassess gradient.","trap":"Dobutamine, milrinone, nitrates and diuretics reduce volume or afterload or increase contractility and worsen obstruction."},"B14_11":{"clue":"High LUS score with posterior losses but normal e' and E/e' 6.8.","mech":"Normal filling pressure excludes cardiogenic edema; dependent loss with aerated anterior fields indicates derecruitment.","confirm":"Optimize aeration with PEEP, positioning or physiotherapy and repeat LUS score before extubation.","trap":"Calling cardiogenic edema because of impaired relaxation; E/A under 1 with low E/e' means filling pressure is not elevated."},"B14_12":{"clue":"Rising antero-lateral B lines during SBT.","mech":"Nondependent regions lose aeration when pulmonary edema develops; dependent regions may improve from diaphragm contraction.","confirm":"Compare antero-lateral B lines before and during the SBT, alongside E/e' changes.","trap":"Assessing only anterior fields or relying on effusion size; these are not accurate predictors."},"B15_1":{"clue":"Severe LV dysfunction before ECMO for ARDS.","mech":"VV ECMO only oxygenates blood; it does not support circulation, so a failing LV cannot pump the oxygenated blood.","confirm":"Quantify LVEF, VTI and RV function and consider VA ECMO or hybrid configurations if output is insufficient.","trap":"Believing VV ECMO unloads the LV or that LV dysfunction contraindicates ECMO."},"B15_2":{"clue":"TR velocity should be measured before VV ECMO.","mech":"VV ECMO drains venous blood and improves oxygenation, decompressing the RV and lowering pulmonary pressures, altering TR jet.","confirm":"Obtain baseline TR velocity and RVSP before cannulation to compare RV recovery later.","trap":"Assuming TR disappears or cannot be measured with TEE; it changes because of circuit decompression."},"B15_3":{"clue":"TR velocity 3.5 m/s and RA pressure 15 mm Hg without pulmonic stenosis.","mech":"The simplified Bernoulli equation converts TR velocity into the RV-RA pressure gradient.","confirm":"RVSP = 15 + 4 x 3.5 squared = 15 + 49 = 64 mm Hg, which equals PASP without pulmonic stenosis.","trap":"Forgetting to add RAP gives 49 mm Hg; using v rather than v squared gives the wrong number."},"B15_4":{"clue":"Femoral-jugular VV ECMO: drainage tip in the IVC about 10 cm below the RA, return tip just inside the RA.","mech":"Separating drainage from return keeps oxygenated return blood from being immediately re-aspirated, minimising recirculation.","confirm":"Use the TEE bicaval view or TTE subcostal view to locate both tips and check distance and freedom from septum and tricuspid valve.","trap":"Placing tips close together at the cavo-atrial junction maximises recirculation rather than mixing."},"B15_5":{"clue":"Dual-lumen bicaval cannula: drainage ports in SVC and IVC, return jet aimed at the tricuspid valve.","mech":"Directing oxygenated return across the tricuspid valve sends it to the RV and lungs instead of back into the drainage ports.","confirm":"Use TEE bicaval and RV inflow views with color Doppler to confirm the return jet crosses the tricuspid valve.","trap":"Directing return toward the SVC or into the hepatic IVC promotes recirculation or hepatic vein injury."},"B15_6":{"clue":"Imaging guidewires and cannulas in both cavae during cannulation.","mech":"The midesophageal bicaval view shows the SVC, RA and IVC in one plane, aligned with cannula paths.","confirm":"Obtain the ME bicaval view at about 90-110 degrees and follow the wire from SVC through RA into IVC.","trap":"Transgastric and aortic valve views do not display both cavae; with TTE, use subcostal and RV inflow-outflow views."},"B15_7":{"clue":"Persistent hypoxemia on VV ECMO with high flow and good oxygenator, cannula tip in the RV.","mech":"A malpositioned dual-lumen cannula brings the drainage port near the return jet, so oxygenated blood is re-drained rather than delivered to the patient.","confirm":"Inject agitated saline into the return limb and watch bubbles enter the drainage port, and check pre-oxygenator saturation.","trap":"Assuming high native cardiac output; that also causes hypoxemia but the malpositioned tip points to recirculation."},"B15_8":{"clue":"Rhythmic chugging of the drainage line with the IVC collapsing around the cannula.","mech":"Excess negative drainage pressure exceeds venous return, so the IVC walls collapse around the cannula and flow falls.","confirm":"Reduce pump speed, give a fluid bolus while watching the IVC and flow on ultrasound, and reassess cannula position.","trap":"Raising pump speed increases negative pressure and worsens collapse; conversion to VA does not address suck-down."},"B15_9":{"clue":"LVOT diameter 2.0 cm, VTI 25 cm, HR 120 with ECMO flow 4 L/min.","mech":"When native output far exceeds ECMO flow, much desaturated venous blood bypasses the oxygenator and dilutes arterial saturation.","confirm":"SV = 3.14 x 1.0 squared x 25 = 78.5 mL; CO = 78.5 x 120 = about 9.4 L/min; consider esmolol to reduce CO.","trap":"Dobutamine increases CO and worsens shunt; sweep gas affects CO2 removal, not oxygenation fraction."},"B15_10":{"clue":"Severe aortic insufficiency before VA ECMO.","mech":"Retrograde aortic flow from VA ECMO increases afterload and worsens AR, causing LV distension and pulmonary edema.","confirm":"Grade AR before cannulation with color, vena contracta and pressure half-time; consider alternative support if severe.","trap":"PE with RV failure and post-MI cardiogenic shock are indications, not contraindications."},"B15_11":{"clue":"On VA ECMO: AV no longer opens, dense LA smoke and a dilating LV.","mech":"Retrograde arterial flow raises LV afterload; if the LV cannot eject, blood stagnates and pressures rise, risking thrombus and edema.","confirm":"Lower ECMO flow under echo until the AV opens; if not possible, vent the LV with IABP, Impella or surgical vent.","trap":"Increasing flow further raises afterload and worsens LV distension; this is not an expected benign finding."},"B15_12":{"clue":"LVEF 30%, LVOT VTI 12 cm and mitral S' 7 cm/s during turndown.","mech":"These values show the LV can generate adequate forward stroke volume when ECMO support is reduced.","confirm":"Verify predictors: LVEF over 25%, VTI over 10 cm, S' over 6 cm/s, no LV dilation and no tamponade.","trap":"Progressive LV dilation, low VTI or a new effusion indicate weaning failure even if EF appears moderate."},"B15_13":{"clue":"RV must be assessed before decannulation from VA ECMO.","mech":"ECMO drains the right heart and offloads the RV, so RV failure may only appear when support is removed.","confirm":"At each turndown step, measure RV size, TAPSE, S', PASP and tricuspid valve function.","trap":"Assuming RV performance does not change or that TR velocity is accurate at full flow; low flow can still mask dysfunction."},"B15_14":{"clue":"Femoral VA ECMO: drainage tip at IVC-RA junction, return in iliac artery or distal aorta.","mech":"Drainage at the junction maximises venous capture; the short femoral return cannula ends in the iliac or distal aorta, delivering retrograde flow.","confirm":"Use TEE bicaval or TTE subcostal views for the drainage tip and visualise the guidewire in the aorta before dilating.","trap":"Expecting the return cannula tip in the thoracic aorta or ascending aorta; femoral return cannulas are short."},"B16_1":{"clue":"SAH with diffuse B lines, preserved sliding, reduced LV function and normal RV.","mech":"Catecholamine surge after SAH injures myocardium, producing LV dysfunction and cardiogenic pulmonary edema.","confirm":"Look for regional wall motion abnormalities beyond a single coronary territory, raised troponin and E/e', and follow recovery on serial echo.","trap":"Lung sliding excludes pneumothorax and a normal RV argues against massive PE despite the new central line."},"B16_2":{"clue":"Temporal window: phased-array 1-3 MHz above the zygoma, marker toward the eyes.","mech":"Low-frequency sound penetrates the thin temporal squama, and orienting along the orbitomeatal line aligns with the basal cisterns.","confirm":"Place the probe anterior and superior to the tragus and adjust until the butterfly-shaped midbrain appears.","trap":"High-frequency linear probes are for ocular imaging, and occipital or mastoid placement does not give the axial basal cistern plane."},"B16_3":{"clue":"Hypoechoic butterfly shape in the basal cistern plane.","mech":"The cerebral peduncles of the midbrain are hypoechoic and surrounded by echogenic cisterns, forming a butterfly.","confirm":"Tilt slightly upward to see the third ventricle as two parallel hyperechoic lines anterior to it.","trap":"The third ventricle is seen as hyperechoic lines, not a butterfly; the pineal is a bright posterior dot."},"B16_4":{"clue":"Third ventricle distance 76 mm from the right and 64 mm from the left.","mech":"With midline shift, the third ventricle moves away from one side and closer to the other; half the difference gives the shift.","confirm":"Midline shift = (76 minus 64)/2 = 6 mm, toward the left (shorter distance side).","trap":"Reporting the raw difference of 12 mm; also, no shift does not exclude raised ICP."},"B16_5":{"clue":"Sharp systolic upstroke with diastolic velocity less than one quarter of peak systolic velocity, no reversal.","mech":"Rising ICP increases distal resistance, reducing diastolic flow and increasing pulsatility.","confirm":"Calculate pulsatility index (PSV minus EDV)/mean velocity and trend it; correlate with ICP and CT.","trap":"Calling it normal; normal diastolic flow is one third to one half of systolic, and reversal indicates ICP above diastolic BP."},"B16_6":{"clue":"Reproducible diastolic reversal in all major intracranial arteries.","mech":"ICP above diastolic BP prevents forward diastolic flow, producing oscillating flow, a sign of irreversible circulatory arrest.","confirm":"Insonate all anterior and posterior circulation vessels, repeat after 30 minutes and integrate with clinical brain death testing.","trap":"Calling it vasospasm or filter artifact; a high wall filter removes low velocities but does not create reversal."},"B16_7":{"clue":"Nonpulsatile forward MCA flow with mean velocity about 60 cm/s on VA ECMO.","mech":"Continuous ECMO flow removes pulsatility, so continuous forward flow is expected and indicates intact cerebral perfusion.","confirm":"Confirm forward flow direction and velocity in both MCAs; clinical examination is confounded by paralysis.","trap":"Interpreting loss of pulsatility as circulatory arrest; on ECMO, absence of forward flow, not pulsatility, defines arrest."},"B16_8":{"clue":"Absent MCA flow without a prior study documenting a window.","mech":"Around 10% of people lack an adequate temporal window, so absent signal may reflect bone, not absent flow.","confirm":"Document windows on an earlier study or find flow in other vessels; insonate all anterior and posterior vessels per protocol.","trap":"Assuming absent flow equals circulatory arrest; it is only valid when flow was previously insonated."},"B16_9":{"clue":"MCA mean velocity 201 cm/s with aliasing on day 7 after SAH.","mech":"Velocity rises when the vessel narrows (vasospasm or stenosis) or when flow increases (hyperemia).","confirm":"Calculate the Lindegaard ratio (MCA/ICA mean velocity) to separate vasospasm from hyperemia and correlate clinically.","trap":"Treating velocity alone as proof of vasospasm; hyperemia also raises velocity without narrowing."},"B16_10":{"clue":"Suspected low-flow state on TCD.","mech":"High scale and wall filters reject low-frequency shifts, so low velocities are filtered out or not displayed.","confirm":"Lower PRF and wall filter, increase gain, and use a 5 mm gate on the proximal MCA.","trap":"Raising the wall filter or enlarging the gate degrades sensitivity, and linear probes cannot penetrate the skull."},"B16_11":{"clue":"Normal MCA waveforms, velocities and pulsatility index in a patient without invasive ICP monitoring.","mech":"Normal waveforms indicate adequate perfusion pressure across the cerebral bed, so significant CPP compromise is unlikely.","confirm":"Repeat TCD serially and correlate with neurological examination and CT when feasible.","trap":"TCD does not assess brainstem function or midline shift; it cannot exclude brain death or structural shift."},"B16_12":{"clue":"TCD shows flow patterns but does not assess brainstem function.","mech":"TCD measures intracranial arterial flow and can show circulatory arrest, but not neurological function.","confirm":"Complete clinical brain death examination and apnea test, then use protocolized insonation of all vessels as an ancillary test.","trap":"Assuming TCD replaces clinical testing or that bilateral MCA insonation alone confirms brain death."},"B17_1":{"clue":"The most sensitive RUQ site for free fluid is the caudal tip of the liver.","mech":"Free fluid tracks along the inferior liver edge toward the right paracolic gutter, often before filling Morison's pouch.","confirm":"Image all three RUQ areas: subdiaphragmatic space, hepatorenal recess and caudal tip of the liver.","trap":"Focusing only on Morison's pouch misses fluid at the liver tip."},"B17_2":{"clue":"Hypotensive trauma patient with an initially negative FAST and no other bleeding source.","mech":"FAST sensitivity is limited; small or early bleeding may be missed, so repeat examination increases detection.","confirm":"Repeat FAST; if two scans are negative but suspicion persists, proceed to DPL or exploratory laparotomy.","trap":"Accepting a single negative FAST, or sending an unstable patient to CT."},"B17_3":{"clue":"Cochrane pooled FAST accuracy: sensitivity 68% and specificity 95%.","mech":"FAST detects free fluid, not organ injury, so injuries without significant hemoperitoneum are missed, lowering sensitivity.","confirm":"Use a positive FAST to proceed but follow a negative FAST with CT or repeat scans.","trap":"Mixing with lung ultrasound pneumothorax data (81/98) or older single-centre studies (78.6/100)."},"B17_4":{"clue":"Fewer than 10% of operators detected less than 400 mL of fluid.","mech":"Free fluid must accumulate before it appears in dependent spaces; average RUQ threshold was about 619 mL.","confirm":"Use Trendelenburg to lower the RUQ threshold and image the pelvis, which detects smaller volumes.","trap":"Assuming small volumes such as 50 mL are reliably detected in the RUQ."},"B17_5":{"clue":"US detects as little as 20 mL of pleural fluid versus about 175 mL on radiograph.","mech":"Ultrasound directly visualises anechoic fluid in dependent recesses, whereas radiographs need enough fluid to blunt costophrenic angles.","confirm":"Image the costophrenic recess above the diaphragm and look for anechoic fluid and the spine sign.","trap":"Supine radiographs need even more fluid, so a normal CXR does not exclude hemothorax."},"B17_6":{"clue":"Thoracic spine visible above the diaphragm (spine sign).","mech":"Aerated lung normally scatters the beam; pleural fluid transmits sound and reveals the spine above the diaphragm.","confirm":"Look for anechoic or echogenic fluid above the diaphragm and quantify; correlate with hemoglobin trend.","trap":"Calling it mirror artifact; normal lung produces a mirror image of the liver, not a visible spine."},"B17_7":{"clue":"Absent sliding but visible B lines from the pleural line.","mech":"B lines arise from the visceral pleura; air between pleural layers prevents their formation.","confirm":"Any B line rules out pneumothorax at that point; confirm by scanning other sites.","trap":"Equating absent sliding with pneumothorax; B lines or lung pulse exclude it locally."},"B17_8":{"clue":"Absent sliding over the whole left hemithorax after field intubation with absent breath sounds.","mech":"Right mainstem intubation prevents ventilation of the left lung, abolishing sliding without pneumothorax.","confirm":"Look for lung pulse, check tube depth and withdraw the tube if necessary, then reassess sliding.","trap":"Placing a chest tube without excluding mainstem intubation; absent sliding is not specific for pneumothorax."},"B17_9":{"clue":"Large left hemothorax with negative subxiphoid view after a stab wound.","mech":"A pericardial laceration can drain blood into the pleural cavity, preventing pericardial accumulation.","confirm":"Pursue further evaluation such as repeat imaging, pericardial window or surgical exploration.","trap":"Trusting a negative pericardial view despite high sensitivity; hemothorax can mask cardiac injury."},"B17_10":{"clue":"Parasternal third to fifth interspaces in a supine patient.","mech":"Air rises to the highest point of the supine chest, around the anterior lower chest near the fifth or sixth rib.","confirm":"Scan parasternal interspaces 3 to 5 and lateral regions; scanning multiple interspaces improves sensitivity.","trap":"Focusing on apical or lateral axillary interspaces misses small anterior pneumothoraces."},"B17_11":{"clue":"LUQ free fluid most commonly between diaphragm and spleen.","mech":"Fluid collects in the subphrenic space before the splenorenal recess because of the phrenicocolic ligament.","confirm":"Start at the posterior axillary line, angle obliquely, and image the subdiaphragmatic space and splenorenal recess.","trap":"Focusing only on the splenorenal recess misses fluid above the spleen."},"B17_12":{"clue":"Trauma arrest with no cardiac motion on subxiphoid ultrasound.","mech":"Absent cardiac motion indicates no meaningful cardiac activity, so resuscitative thoracotomy rarely succeeds.","confirm":"Confirm absence of motion in an adequate subxiphoid or parasternal view and consider stopping resuscitation.","trap":"Assuming draining tamponade changes outcome; mortality was 100% with no motion."},"B18_1":{"clue":"16 segments: 8 normal, 4 hypokinetic and 4 akinetic.","mech":"WMSI averages segmental scores; higher values reflect larger infarct size and greater dysfunction.","confirm":"WMSI = (8 x 1 + 4 x 2 + 4 x 3)/16 = 28/16 = 1.75, above the 1.7 high-risk threshold.","trap":"Dividing by 17 segments or miscounting scores; only visualised segments are counted."},"B18_2":{"clue":"Basal inferoseptal, mid inferior and apical inferior hypokinesis.","mech":"The RCA usually supplies inferior and inferoseptal segments via the posterior descending artery.","confirm":"Map abnormalities on the 17-segment model and look for RV involvement suggesting proximal RCA occlusion.","trap":"The LAD supplies anterior and septal segments; the LCx supplies lateral segments."},"B18_3":{"clue":"Flail leaflet with a mobile mass moving between LV and LA days after small inferior MI.","mech":"The posteromedial papillary muscle has single blood supply from the posterior descending artery, making it vulnerable to infarction and rupture.","confirm":"Use TEE to identify the ruptured head and chordae and quantify MR; arrange urgent surgery.","trap":"The anterolateral papillary muscle has dual LAD and LCx supply and ruptures less often."},"B18_4":{"clue":"High-velocity LV to RV jet in the apical septum with hypercontractile noninfarcted LV.","mech":"Infarcted septal myocardium ruptures, creating a left-to-right shunt and pulmonary overflow.","confirm":"Scan the entire septum from base to apex with color Doppler and measure shunt ratio Qp/Qs.","trap":"Papillary muscle rupture causes MR into the LA, not a ventricular jet; LVOT obstruction produces late-peaking gradients."},"B18_5":{"clue":"Effusion with intrapericardial echogenic material and tamponade after anterior STEMI.","mech":"Subacute free wall rupture leaks blood into the pericardium, forming thrombus and tamponade.","confirm":"Look for hemopericardium, thrombus and a small slit-like defect; arrange urgent surgery.","trap":"Post-MI pericarditis effusions are small and rarely cause tamponade."},"B18_6":{"clue":"Apical infarct with basal hyperkinesis, SAM and a new murmur while dobutamine is started.","mech":"Hyperkinetic basal segments narrow the LVOT and drag the mitral leaflet into it; beta-1 stimulation increases contractility and worsens obstruction.","confirm":"Use CW Doppler for a late-peaking LVOT gradient and color for posterior MR; stop inotropes, give volume and a pure vasoconstrictor, and recheck the gradient.","trap":"Assuming pump failure because of the infarct; inotropes worsen obstruction, and SAM-related MR is not papillary muscle rupture."},"B18_7":{"clue":"Inferior STEMI with hypotension, raised JVP, clear lungs, severe hypoxemia and interatrial septum bowing into the LA.","mech":"RV infarction raises RA pressure above LA pressure, opening a PFO and shunting deoxygenated blood right to left.","confirm":"Inject agitated saline and look for bubbles crossing into the left heart within three beats; measure TAPSE and IVC size.","trap":"Attributing hypoxemia to LV failure or PE; clear lungs and RA-LA septal bowing point to shunting."},"B18_8":{"clue":"Post-MI outpouching with a narrow neck and thrombus.","mech":"Incomplete free wall rupture is contained by pericardium and organized hematoma, forming a narrow-necked false aneurysm prone to rupture.","confirm":"Measure neck-to-body diameter ratio (narrow neck, ratio under about 0.5), look for abrupt myocardial discontinuity and flow across the neck with color Doppler.","trap":"True aneurysms also contain thrombus and are dyskinetic, but they have a wide neck and walls of thinned myocardium."},"B18_9":{"clue":"Takotsubo requires exclusion of an acute culprit coronary lesion.","mech":"Catecholamine surge produces regional dysfunction beyond a single coronary territory that echo cannot reliably separate from ACS.","confirm":"Perform coronary angiography and follow recovery of wall motion on serial echo over days to weeks.","trap":"It represents 1-3% of suspected STEMI, apical ballooning is most common, and coexisting CAD does not exclude it."},"B18_10":{"clue":"Ischemic cascade timing: wall motion abnormality before ECG changes and pain.","mech":"Ischemia impairs perfusion, metabolism, then diastolic and systolic function before electrical changes and symptoms appear.","confirm":"Look for new regional wall motion abnormalities in the acute setting even when ECG is nondiagnostic.","trap":"Assuming ECG changes or chest pain occur first; necrosis is the last step."},"B18_11":{"clue":"Dilated RV with preserved RV apical motion and a hyperdynamic LV.","mech":"Acute afterload rise in PE strains the RV free wall but the apex is pulled by the hyperdynamic LV; LV underfilling reflects reduced RV output.","confirm":"Check TR velocity (usually PASP under 60 mm Hg), IVC plethora and DVT on compression ultrasound.","trap":"Inferior hypokinesis with RV dilation suggests RV infarction from RCA occlusion, not PE."},"B18_12":{"clue":"Thinned segment moving outward in systole.","mech":"Scarred, noncontractile myocardium is pushed outward by LV systolic pressure, defining dyskinesis.","confirm":"Score 4 in ASE 2015: 1 normal or hyperkinetic, 2 hypokinetic, 3 akinetic, 4 dyskinetic or aneurysmal.","trap":"Scoring as akinesis (3), which is absent thickening without outward motion; older schemes used 5 for aneurysm."},"B19_1":{"clue":"Color scale of 15 cm/s producing mosaic flow across normally opening leaflets.","mech":"With a low Nyquist limit, normal inflow velocities exceed the limit and alias, mimicking turbulence.","confirm":"Reset Nyquist to 50-70 cm/s and reassess; use CW Doppler and leaflet motion to evaluate stenosis.","trap":"Calling stenosis or regurgitation from aliasing at low scale; normal opening and laminar flow at the correct scale exclude obstruction."},"B19_2":{"clue":"Systolic flow reversal in more than one pulmonary vein.","mech":"Severe MR raises LA pressure during systole above pulmonary venous pressure, reversing flow in multiple veins.","confirm":"Sample PW Doppler 1 cm into at least two pulmonary veins, ideally with TEE, and look for systolic reversal.","trap":"Systolic blunting is moderate and nonspecific; single-vein reversal from an eccentric jet can occur with less than severe MR."},"B19_3":{"clue":"Peak aortic velocity 4.5 m/s.","mech":"The simplified Bernoulli equation converts velocity to pressure gradient when proximal velocity is negligible.","confirm":"Gradient = 4 x 4.5 squared = 4 x 20.25 = 81 mm Hg; align CW beam coaxially from multiple windows.","trap":"Forgetting to square the velocity; off-axis alignment underestimates the gradient."},"B19_4":{"clue":"LVOT diameter 2.0 cm, LVOT VTI 20 cm and AV VTI 80 cm.","mech":"The continuity equation assumes flow through the LVOT equals flow through the aortic valve.","confirm":"CSA LVOT = 0.785 x 2.0 squared = 3.14 cm2; AVA = (3.14 x 20)/80 = 0.8 cm2, severe AS.","trap":"Using radius instead of diameter in the formula or sampling LVOT VTI at a different level from diameter measurement."},"B19_5":{"clue":"LVOT diameter in mid-systole, inner edge to inner edge, within 2-5 mm of hinge points.","mech":"The LVOT is largest and most circular in mid-systole near the annulus; diameter errors are squared in area.","confirm":"Measure in zoomed PLAX or ME long-axis view, parallel to the valve plane, in mid-systole.","trap":"Measuring in diastole, at the sinotubular junction or leading edge to leading edge introduces squared errors."},"B19_6":{"clue":"Peak velocity 4.2 m/s in a septic, hyperdynamic patient but DI of 0.30.","mech":"High flow inflates velocity and gradient across a moderately narrowed valve; DI corrects for flow because both VTIs rise.","confirm":"DI = LVOT VTI/AV VTI = 30/100 = 0.30, above 0.25 so not severe; reassess when flow normalises.","trap":"Grading AS by velocity or gradient alone; these are flow-dependent and overestimate severity in high-output states."},"B19_7":{"clue":"Anterior leaflet contacting the septum, posterior MR jet and late-peaking dagger LVOT signal after MV repair.","mech":"An underfilled, hyperdynamic LV with tachycardia drags the anterior leaflet into the LVOT, causing late systolic obstruction and MR.","confirm":"Measure LVOT gradient with CW Doppler and evaluate SAM on TEE; give volume, stop inotropes and add a vasoconstrictor or beta-blocker.","trap":"AS gives a rounded, earlier-peaking profile; a subaortic membrane is fixed; papillary rupture gives a flail leaflet, not SAM."},"B19_8":{"clue":"LVEF 20% with calcified AV, peak velocity 3.2 m/s, mean gradient 24 mm Hg, AVA 0.8 cm2.","mech":"Low flow reduces velocity and gradient despite a small valve area, producing low-flow, low-gradient AS.","confirm":"Calculate DI and consider low-dose dobutamine stress to distinguish true severe from pseudo-severe AS.","trap":"Grading as moderate from gradient; the continuity area is relatively flow-independent and remains valid."},"B19_9":{"clue":"Mitral pressure half-time 275 ms in rheumatic MS.","mech":"A narrower orifice prolongs the time for the LA-LV gradient to fall by half.","confirm":"MVA = 220/PHT = 220/275 = 0.8 cm2, severe (under 1.0 cm2); measure PHT on the mid E-wave slope.","trap":"Using the steep early part of the slope or applying PHT outside rheumatic MS."},"B19_10":{"clue":"Heavy annular calcification extending into the leaflets with a high transmitral gradient.","mech":"Calcific MS narrows the annulus and base rather than commissures, and PHT reflects chamber compliance more than orifice size.","confirm":"Use mean gradient, continuity equation or planimetry with 3D TEE when feasible.","trap":"Applying the 220/PHT formula, validated only in rheumatic MS and confounded by LV and LA compliance."},"B19_11":{"clue":"Rheumatic MS with AF at 130/min and mean gradient 14 mm Hg.","mech":"Tachycardia shortens diastole and increases flow, raising the gradient independent of valve area.","confirm":"Average the mean gradient over 5 beats and recheck after rate control; correlate with planimetry or PHT.","trap":"Assuming gradients are rate-independent or measuring only the longest RR interval."},"B19_12":{"clue":"Thickened, restricted tricuspid leaflets with a dilated IVC and RA in carcinoid disease.","mech":"Low-pressure right-sided inflow means even small gradients indicate significant stenosis.","confirm":"Measure mean gradient over 5 beats; at least 5 mm Hg, inflow VTI over 60 cm and PHT at least 190 ms support severe TS.","trap":"Using left-sided thresholds or the 1.5 cm2 area; severe TS is 1 cm2 or less, and coexisting TR increases the gradient."},"B19_13":{"clue":"RVSP 90 mm Hg with RVOT or pulmonary valve peak velocity 4.3 m/s.","mech":"A pulmonary valve gradient means RV pressure exceeds PA pressure, so RVSP overestimates PASP.","confirm":"Gradient = 4 x 4.3 squared = about 74 mm Hg, severe PS; PASP = RVSP minus PS gradient.","trap":"Attributing high RVSP to pulmonary hypertension without interrogating the RVOT."},"B19_14":{"clue":"Flail leaflet with an eccentric, wall-hugging jet occupying only about 20% of the LA.","mech":"Coanda effect makes eccentric jets cling to the wall and lose apparent area, underestimating severity.","confirm":"Measure vena contracta and PISA EROA, and check pulmonary vein flow reversal.","trap":"Grading by jet area or adjusting gain or Nyquist to fill the atrium; a flail leaflet itself is specific for severe MR."},"B19_15":{"clue":"PISA radius 1.0 cm, aliasing velocity 40 cm/s, MR peak velocity 500 cm/s, MR VTI 150 cm.","mech":"Flow through the hemispheric convergence zone equals flow through the regurgitant orifice.","confirm":"Flow = 2 x 3.14 x 1 squared x 40 = 251 mL/s; EROA = 251/500 = 0.50 cm2; RVol = 0.50 x 150 = 75 mL, severe.","trap":"Forgetting to square the radius or using mean rather than peak MR velocity."},"B19_16":{"clue":"Short AR PHT in septic shock with low SVR.","mech":"Low aortic diastolic pressure and high LV diastolic pressure equalise faster, shortening PHT independent of AR severity.","confirm":"Use vena contracta, jet width ratio and abdominal aortic holodiastolic reversal rather than PHT.","trap":"Assuming short PHT always means severe AR; it is load-dependent."},"B19_17":{"clue":"Holodiastolic reversal in the abdominal aorta.","mech":"Large regurgitant volume reverses diastolic flow even far from the valve, which is specific for severe AR.","confirm":"Sample PW Doppler in the proximal abdominal aorta and look for end-diastolic reversal velocity.","trap":"Brief early-diastolic reversal in the descending aorta is normal and can occur in stiff aortas."},"B19_18":{"clue":"LVOT diameter 2.2 cm, VTI 30 cm; mitral annulus 3.0 cm, VTI 7 cm.","mech":"LVOT flow includes forward plus regurgitant volume; mitral inflow equals forward flow without significant MR.","confirm":"SV LVOT = 0.785 x 2.2 squared x 30 = 114 mL; SV mitral = 3.14 x 1.5 squared x 7 = 50 mL; RVol = 64 mL, RF = 57%.","trap":"Using radius incorrectly or applying the method with significant MR."},"B19_19":{"clue":"Flail P2 segment of the posterior mitral leaflet.","mech":"With prolapse or flail, the regurgitant jet is directed away from the affected leaflet.","confirm":"Use TEE color Doppler to follow jet direction and quantify with PISA or vena contracta.","trap":"Expecting the jet toward the posterior leaflet; that happens with tethering, not flail."},"B19_20":{"clue":"Severe TR, annulus 42 mm, dilated IVC and diastolic-only septal flattening.","mech":"Severe TR causes RV volume overload, flattening the septum in diastole but not systole.","confirm":"Look for systolic hepatic vein reversal and measure vena contracta and EROA for TR severity.","trap":"Calling pressure overload, which flattens the septum throughout systole and diastole."},"B20_1":{"clue":"Persistent S. aureus bacteremia with prosthetic valve and dialysis catheter despite negative TTE.","mech":"TTE has limited resolution for prosthetic valves and devices because of acoustic shadowing.","confirm":"Perform TEE to look for vegetations, abscess or prosthetic dehiscence.","trap":"Accepting negative TTE as exclusion; sensitivity for prosthetic or device IE is only about 40-70%."},"B20_2":{"clue":"Tricuspid vegetation on TEE plus fever, IV drug use and Janeway lesions.","mech":"Vegetation is a major criterion; fever, predisposition and vascular phenomena are three minor criteria.","confirm":"One major plus three minor criteria equals definite IE by modified Duke criteria.","trap":"Waiting for blood cultures; echo evidence counts as a major criterion even before cultures return."},"B20_3":{"clue":"TTE sensitivity only about 40-70% for prosthetic or device IE.","mech":"Prosthetic materials shadow and reverberate, hiding vegetations and abscesses on TTE.","confirm":"Use TEE when TTE is negative and suspicion is high, especially with prosthetic valves or devices.","trap":"TTE specificity for native IE is about 90%, not 50%; TEE is less sensitive for prosthetic than native valves."},"B20_4":{"clue":"Repeat TEE in 3 to 7 days if suspicion persists.","mech":"Small vegetations may enlarge and become visible over days, and TEE is not 100% sensitive.","confirm":"Repeat TEE after 3-7 days, or immediately if clinical deterioration suggests complications.","trap":"Assuming TEE is perfectly sensitive or waiting until completing antibiotics."},"B20_5":{"clue":"12 mm mobile vegetation on the anterior mitral leaflet.","mech":"Large, mobile left-sided vegetations carry higher embolic risk, especially on the anterior mitral leaflet.","confirm":"Measure vegetation length and mobility on TEE and assess valve function, abscess and emboli by imaging.","trap":"Assuming surgery requires a documented embolus or size above 20 mm; at least 10 mm is already a high-risk feature."},"B20_6":{"clue":"Stalked, frond-like mass on the aortic side of the aortic valve with normal valve function and negative cultures.","mech":"Papillary fibroelastomas are benign tumours on the downstream side of valves, prone to embolism.","confirm":"Use TEE to define stalk and location; consider surgical excision if mobile or embolic.","trap":"Vegetations sit on the upstream side and damage the valve; Lambl's excrescences are thin filiform strands."},"B20_7":{"clue":"Verrucous lesions at the mitral leaflet tips in SLE with antiphospholipid syndrome and negative cultures.","mech":"Nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis forms sterile platelet-fibrin deposits on valves in autoimmune disease.","confirm":"Check antiphospholipid antibodies, exclude infection with cultures and use TEE to assess lesion size.","trap":"Marantic endocarditis is associated with malignancy; infective vegetations usually cause valve damage."},"B20_8":{"clue":"Vegetations on the ventricular (upstream) side of aortic cusps in the path of the regurgitant jet.","mech":"Vegetations form in low-pressure zones adjacent to high-velocity jets on the upstream surface of regurgitant valves.","confirm":"Use TEE long-axis and short-axis views to locate vegetations and look for perforation or abscess.","trap":"Papillary fibroelastomas sit on the downstream (aortic) side; vegetations favour the upstream side."},"B20_9":{"clue":"New heart block with heterogeneous thickening of the aortic annulus and intervalvular fibrosa on TEE.","mech":"Infection extends from the valve into the periannular tissue near the conduction system, forming an abscess that disrupts AV conduction.","confirm":"Image the annulus in multiple TEE planes for echolucent or heterogeneous perivalvular areas and arrange early surgery.","trap":"Calling it post-inflammatory thickening; new heart block in aortic IE is an abscess until proven otherwise, and TTE often misses it."},"B20_10":{"clue":"Echolucent perivalvular cavity that expands in systole with color flow into and out of it.","mech":"An abscess that ruptures into the lumen becomes a pulsatile pseudoaneurysm communicating with the LVOT or aorta.","confirm":"Use color Doppler to show systolic inflow and diastolic outflow and look for fistula into adjacent chambers.","trap":"A closed abscess has no flow communication; a pericardial collection or coronary sinus does not expand with systolic flow."},"B20_11":{"clue":"Rocking mechanical mitral prosthesis with severe periprosthetic regurgitation and bacteremia.","mech":"Mechanical valve infection targets the sewing ring-tissue junction, causing abscess and suture dehiscence that lets the ring move.","confirm":"Use TEE with color Doppler to locate the paravalvular jet and extent of dehiscence; refer for surgery.","trap":"Leaflet destruction is typical of bioprostheses; pannus is fixed and immobile; new dehiscence is a major Duke criterion."},"B20_12":{"clue":"Pacemaker with bacteremia and negative TTE.","mech":"TTE cannot reliably image leads in the SVC and RA because of shadowing, and lead vegetations resemble thrombus.","confirm":"Perform TEE imaging the SVC, RA and RV lead courses; repeat TEE or use PET-CT if suspicion persists.","trap":"Assuming oscillation distinguishes vegetation from thrombus or labelling the Eustachian valve as a vegetation."},"B21_1":{"clue":"End-diastolic RV:LV area ratio over 0.6 with systemic congestion in ARDS.","mech":"High PVR in ARDS dilates the RV; when dilation causes venous congestion, the RV is failing.","confirm":"Measure end-diastolic RV and LV areas in a TEE or apical 4-chamber view and look for raised CVP, IVC plethora and hepatic congestion.","trap":"Using end-systolic ratios or TAPSE alone; TAPSE marks dysfunction but is not the dilation criterion."},"B21_2":{"clue":"Shock in ARDS: sepsis accounts for about half of cases.","mech":"Pneumonia causes both ARDS and sepsis, so vasodilatory circulatory failure is the most frequent mechanism.","confirm":"Use echo to separate vasodilatory shock from acute cor pulmonale and LV dysfunction.","trap":"Assuming acute cor pulmonale dominates; it is the main mechanism only in unstable patients without sepsis."},"B21_3":{"clue":"PPV 16% with a dilated RV enlarging during inspiration, dilated IVC and no SVC variation.","mech":"Positive pressure raises RV afterload in inspiration, reducing RV output and causing PPV without preload dependence.","confirm":"Check RV size and septal motion, absence of SVC collapse and high CVP; adjust ventilation, control CO2 and consider prone positioning.","trap":"Giving fluid for high PPV; in acute cor pulmonale PPV is a false positive and fluid worsens RV failure."},"B21_4":{"clue":"Delta SVC on TEE is much more specific than sensitive.","mech":"The SVC is intrathoracic, so its collapse reflects transmitted pleural pressure on a compliant, underfilled vein.","confirm":"Measure SVC diameter in the upper esophageal view with M-mode and calculate (Dmax minus Dmin)/Dmax.","trap":"Thinking it needs high tidal volumes or a subcostal window; most patients studied had tidal volumes under 8 mL/kg."},"B21_5":{"clue":"End-systolic paradoxical septal motion with a D-shaped LV.","mech":"RV systolic pressure overload prolongs RV ejection past LV ejection, pushing the septum leftward at end-systole.","confirm":"Use short-axis view with M-mode or frame-by-frame to time septal flattening at end-systole.","trap":"Volume overload causes diastolic septal shift, not end-systolic paradox."},"B21_6":{"clue":"Driving pressure 18 cm H2O or higher is a risk factor for ACP in ARDS.","mech":"High driving pressure compresses alveolar vessels and raises PVR, increasing RV afterload.","confirm":"Check plateau minus PEEP and other risk factors: pneumonia, PaO2/FiO2 under 150 and PaCO2 at least 48.","trap":"The relevant PaO2/FiO2 threshold is under 150, not 250, and PEEP or tidal volume alone are not in the score."},"B21_7":{"clue":"Thermodilution devices are inaccurate on ECMO.","mech":"The circuit removes and returns blood, diverting the indicator and distorting thermodilution curves.","confirm":"Measure LVOT VTI and diameter with PW Doppler to estimate SV and CO serially.","trap":"Assuming pulse contour is contraindicated or that TTE cannot obtain VTI; the issue is indicator loss."},"B21_8":{"clue":"ARDS with pulmonary hypertension, dilated RV, normal LVEF and moderate vasopressors.","mech":"VV ECMO improves oxygenation and CO2 removal, lowering PVR and unloading the RV.","confirm":"Reassess RV size and PASP after VV initiation; conversion to VA is rarely needed.","trap":"Assuming pulmonary hypertension or any vasopressor need mandates VA ECMO; ECCO2R does not fix hypoxemia."},"B21_9":{"clue":"Severe cardiogenic shock with very low CO and severely reduced LVEF on high-dose support.","mech":"VV ECMO cannot support the circulation; profound LV failure needs arterial flow support.","confirm":"Measure LVEF, VTI and cardiac output and assess vasopressor and inotrope needs before choosing VA.","trap":"RV failure from pulmonary hypertension and hypercapnia are usually relieved by VV ECMO."},"B21_10":{"clue":"Low cardiac index decreases intrapulmonary shunt.","mech":"Lower flow reduces perfusion of non-ventilated lung, reducing shunt fraction and making oxygenation look better.","confirm":"Interpret PaO2/FiO2 alongside CO, SvO2 and PFO status.","trap":"Low venous oxygen and PFO shunting worsen oxygenation and overestimate severity."},"B22_1":{"clue":"Large tissue-like echotexture resembling liver.","mech":"Alveoli filled with exudate transmit sound like solid tissue, producing hepatization in lobar or translobar consolidation.","confirm":"Look for air bronchograms and absence of a shred sign; correlate with clinical infection markers.","trap":"Nontranslobar consolidation shows a shred sign; atelectasis and interstitial syndrome do not look liver-like."},"B22_2":{"clue":"Dynamic air bronchograms in a consolidation.","mech":"Air moving within patent bronchi during breathing shows the airway is open, favouring pneumonia over resorptive atelectasis.","confirm":"Observe air bronchograms moving with respiration and look for a lung pulse.","trap":"Static air bronchograms occur in both pneumonia and atelectasis; effusion is nonspecific."},"B22_3":{"clue":"Bilateral B lines with spared areas and subpleural consolidations.","mech":"ARDS causes heterogeneous inflammatory injury, sparing some areas while damaging others and thickening the pleura.","confirm":"Look for pleural thickening over 2 mm, reduced sliding, lung pulse and normal or near-normal filling pressure.","trap":"Homogeneous laser-like B lines, thin regular pleura and bilateral effusions favour cardiogenic edema."},"B22_4":{"clue":"Diffuse B lines with LVEF 60% and average E/e' 16.","mech":"Elevated E/e' reflects raised LV filling pressure, driving hydrostatic pulmonary edema.","confirm":"Average E/e' over 14 indicates elevated LVFP; check LA volume and TR velocity for corroboration.","trap":"Assuming E/e' is invalid with normal EF; it is specifically needed in preserved EF."},"B22_5":{"clue":"B lines in 4 zones, left effusion, normal LV and IVC 18 mm.","mech":"The score weights findings favouring hydrostatic edema and subtracts for a small IVC that argues against congestion.","confirm":"Score = 3 + 4 + 0 minus 2 = 5, between the ARDS cutoff (3 or less) and CPE cutoff (6 or more).","trap":"Forgetting the IVC subtraction or scoring normal LV as positive."},"B22_6":{"clue":"Score at least 6: sensitivity 0.30, specificity 0.98.","mech":"High specificity means few false positives, so a positive score reliably rules in CPE.","confirm":"Use a score of 6 or higher to rule in CPE and a score of 3 or less to suggest ARDS.","trap":"Using it to rule out CPE; low sensitivity means many CPE patients score below 6."},"B22_7":{"clue":"Kigali modification allows lung ultrasound as the imaging criterion.","mech":"In resource-limited settings, LUS can document bilateral loss of aeration without CXR or CT.","confirm":"Apply LUS findings of bilateral B lines and consolidations with oxygenation criteria per Kigali.","trap":"Berlin requires CXR or CT; Montreux is the neonatal definition without LUS."},"B22_8":{"clue":"Homogeneous bilateral B lines with irregularly thickened pleura without ARDS history.","mech":"Pulmonary fibrosis thickens interlobular septa and pleura, producing B lines and irregular pleura.","confirm":"Review history and CT for interstitial lung disease and compare with prior imaging.","trap":"CPE has a thin regular pleura; ARDS is not the only cause of irregular pleura."},"B22_9":{"clue":"B-line predominance in multiple lung fields outperforms CXR, clinical examination and NT-proBNP.","mech":"B lines reflect extravascular lung water directly, whereas other markers are indirect and nonspecific.","confirm":"Scan multiple lung zones for more than 2 B lines per field bilaterally.","trap":"Assuming BNP or CXR reliably rule out CPE; their accuracy is only around 50-60%."},"B22_10":{"clue":"Interstitial syndrome with large bilateral pleural effusions.","mech":"Raised hydrostatic pressure in left heart failure causes both interstitial edema and transudative effusions.","confirm":"Look for elevated E/e', dilated LA and IVC plethora to confirm hydrostatic mechanism.","trap":"Subpleural consolidations and lung pulse favour ARDS; absent effusions argue against CPE."},"B23_1":{"clue":"Mini-fluid challenge: 50 mL crystalloid over 10 seconds, VTI change about 9%.","mech":"A small, rapid bolus transiently increases preload; a preload-dependent ventricle increases stroke volume measurably.","confirm":"Measure LVOT VTI before and immediately after the bolus; a rise of about 9% predicts responsiveness.","trap":"Using 250 or 500 mL bolus thresholds; the mini challenge relies on a small volume."},"B23_2":{"clue":"Passive ventilation, IVC 2.2 cm at inspiration and 1.8 cm at expiration.","mech":"Positive pressure distends a compliant IVC in inspiration when the venous system is underfilled.","confirm":"Distensibility = (2.2 minus 1.8)/1.8 x 100 = 22%, above the 18% threshold.","trap":"Dividing by Dmax (gives 18%) or using the mean denominator threshold of 12%."},"B23_3":{"clue":"Spontaneously breathing septic patient: IVC collapsibility over 48%.","mech":"Negative inspiratory pressure collapses an underfilled IVC more, suggesting volume responsiveness.","confirm":"Collapsibility = (Dmax expiration minus Dmin inspiration)/Dmax expiration; values over 48% favour response.","trap":"Applying 12% or 18% distensibility thresholds, which are for passively ventilated patients."},"B23_4":{"clue":"Septic shock with ARDS: delta LVOT velocity 14% and delta IVC 13%.","mech":"With high risk of fluid overload, a strict threshold of 18% maximises specificity before giving fluid.","confirm":"Use the 18% threshold for both indices; consider PLR or mini-fluid challenge before bolus.","trap":"Using lower thresholds of 10-12%, acceptable only when risk of lung water overload is low."},"B23_5":{"clue":"Aortic peak velocity variation best sensitivity, SVC variation best specificity.","mech":"Aortic velocity reflects SV changes directly, while SVC collapse requires significant underfilling.","confirm":"Use aortic velocity variation to screen and SVC collapse to confirm fluid responsiveness.","trap":"Assuming IVC distensibility or PLR performed best in that study."},"B23_6":{"clue":"After 3 L, normal LVEDA but nearly obliterated end-systolic cavity and minimal velocity variation.","mech":"Low SVR allows near-complete emptying despite adequate filling, producing hypotension.","confirm":"Check LVEDA (normal), minimal respiratory variation and normal or high VTI; start vasopressor.","trap":"Assuming persistent hypovolemia; end-systolic obliteration with normal LVEDA indicates vasodilatation."},"B23_7":{"clue":"Peak instantaneous LVOT gradient at least 50 mm Hg on CW Doppler.","mech":"SAM narrows the LVOT in late systole; gradients of 50 mm Hg or more are hemodynamically significant.","confirm":"Align CW Doppler through the LVOT and measure peak late-peaking velocity; 3.5 m/s equals about 50 mm Hg.","trap":"PW velocity of 1.5 m/s or an early rounded envelope do not define obstruction; VTI variation measures preload response."},"B23_8":{"clue":"Dobutamine causing more hypotension and tachycardia with SAM and late-peaking dagger LVOT profile.","mech":"Inotropy and tachycardia shrink the LV cavity and worsen SAM, increasing obstruction and lowering output.","confirm":"Stop dobutamine, give fluid, slow the heart rate and use a pure vasoconstrictor; recheck the LVOT gradient.","trap":"Increasing inotropes or using milrinone worsens obstruction; diuretics reduce preload and worsen it."},"B23_9":{"clue":"SAM with mitral regurgitation directed posteriorly.","mech":"The anterior leaflet is pulled into the LVOT, creating a coaptation gap so the jet goes posteriorly into the LA.","confirm":"Use color Doppler to show a posteriorly directed jet and CW for late-peaking LVOT gradient.","trap":"Anterior jets suggest posterior leaflet prolapse; central jets suggest annular dilatation."},"B23_10":{"clue":"Post-intubation hypotension in COPD with absent sliding, B lines and lung pulse, barcode sign, plethoric IVC and small ventricles.","mech":"Tension pneumothorax raises intrathoracic pressure, preventing venous return and compressing the heart.","confirm":"Search for a lung point if time allows, but proceed to immediate needle decompression in tension physiology.","trap":"Giving fluid or vasopressors; a plethoric IVC with small ventricles signals obstruction, not hypovolemia."},"B23_11":{"clue":"First branch point: high or low cardiac output.","mech":"High output with hypercontractility suggests vasodilatory shock, while low output suggests underfilling or obstruction.","confirm":"Measure LVOT VTI and cardiac output, then reassess after volume; if low output persists, look for LVOTO or pneumothorax.","trap":"Starting with lung sliding or IVC alone; cardiac output determines the next diagnostic path."},"B23_12":{"clue":"Intubated patient with poor TTE windows and suspected endocarditis with possible aortic root abscess.","mech":"TEE gives high-resolution images close to the heart, overcoming poor windows and detecting abscess.","confirm":"Perform TEE with focus on the aortic root, annulus and valve leaflets.","trap":"TTE is enough for initial qualitative LV or IVC assessment; TEE is for detailed or complicated questions."},"B24_1":{"clue":"No palpable pulse but coordinated ventricular contraction on subcostal view.","mech":"Contraction exists but generates too little pressure to produce a palpable pulse, defining pseudo-PEA.","confirm":"Confirm coordinated motion on a brief pulse check and consider arterial line or Doppler flow; treat as low-flow state.","trap":"True PEA shows cardiac standstill; fine VF shows quivering, not coordinated contraction."},"B24_2":{"clue":"Pooled values: sensitivity 95%, specificity 80%, negative LR 0.06.","mech":"Cardiac motion predicts ROSC well; absent motion makes ROSC unlikely, reflected in a low negative LR.","confirm":"Use focused echo during brief pulse checks to identify motion and support decisions with clinical context.","trap":"Reversing sensitivity and specificity or assuming cardiac standstill alone mandates stopping resuscitation."}};
